"""Self-contained Chapter 8 teaching, with original explanations and exact source pages.

The replays identify book positions or original simplifying illustrations.
A displayed defensive reply is not uniquely forced unless the explanation says so.
"""
from core.primer_chapter8 import EXAMPLES
from core.primer_guides_chapter1 import check, guide, section

GUIDES = {}


def teach(key, pages, goals, sections, questions, pitfalls, recap):
    lesson_id = 'ch8-' + key
    checks = [check(f'{key}-{i}', prompt,
                    (answer, True, explanation), (wrong, False, correction))
              for i, (prompt, answer, explanation, wrong, correction) in enumerate(questions, 1)]
    GUIDES[lesson_id] = guide(pages, goals, [section(title, body) for title, body in sections],
                            EXAMPLES[lesson_id], pitfalls, checks, recap)


teach('active-king', (261, 262),
    ('Give the endgame king a concrete job.', 'Balance king activity against remaining checks and pawn races.'),
    (
        ('Why the king changes roles', '''In a crowded middlegame, bringing the king forward can expose it to a mating attack. After queens and many pieces disappear, the balance often changes: the king becomes a powerful short-range attacker and defender. A king can protect a pawn, capture an enemy pawn, take an entry square, or push the other king away. Count it as part of your working army. Leaving it on its starting rank without a safety reason may mean trying to win with one useful piece missing.'''),
        ('Choose a destination before a route', '''Centralization is a means to a job. In the replay, White’s king on e4 goes to d5 so it can lead the e3 pawn and control e6. The pawn follows only when its escort is ready. With targets on the edge, the best king destination may be far from the centre. First name the target or promotion square, then choose a safe route. Check every intended square against the opponent’s king, pawn attacks, and the lines of any remaining rook, bishop or queen.'''),
        ('Calculate the opponent’s useful reply', '''Black also gets a turn and can activate its own king or start a pawn race. The illustrated ...Kf6 and ...Kg5 show one legal development of the position; they are not a proof that White wins against every defence. Before Kd5, pause and count how soon Black’s h-pawn can promote if ignored. Then ask whether your king can both support the e-pawn and return if necessary. An active king that goes to the wrong wing can be beautifully centralized and still too late.'''),
        ('Use the plan without switching calculation off', '''The chapter stresses plans because a small number of pieces makes their long-term jobs easier to see. That does not make tactics disappear. Before each king move, inspect checks and captures, and after each pawn move recalculate the race from the new position. A practical routine is: identify the urgent threat, select the king’s job, check the path, then name the opponent’s best resistance. Replay from both sides and explain what each king gains with its move. This turns “activate the king” into a usable decision process.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('What makes a central king useful?', 'Its actual access to targets, escort squares and safe entry routes.', 'The destination must solve a position-specific job.', 'Its distance from the centre alone.', 'A centralized king can still be too far from the important pawn race.'),
        ('Does the sample ...Kg5 prove White’s whole ending is won?', 'No; it is one illustrative reply and other resistance needs calculation.', 'The line demonstrates an escorting method, not a complete game-theoretic proof.', 'Yes; a legal replay proves every defence loses.', 'Legality establishes possible play, not the best defensive result.'),
    ),
    ('Do not walk into a surviving rook’s checking line.', 'A king journey must include the opponent’s pawn race in its timing.'),
    ('Name the king’s job.', 'Check the route square by square.', 'Recount races after every irreversible pawn move.'))

teach('breakthrough', (262, 263),
    ('Create a passed pawn by deflecting pawn barriers.', 'Calculate capture branches and promotion timing before sacrificing.'),
    (
        ('A temporary material loss can buy a road', '''A passed pawn has no enemy pawn ahead on its own file or an adjacent file that can stop it by advancing or capturing. Three pawns facing three pawns may look completely blocked, but captures can change their files. A breakthrough deliberately gives up pawns so that one survivor obtains a clear road. Its value comes from promotion timing, not from the pawn count immediately after the sacrifice. Do not call the idea successful until you have followed the survivor to the last rank.'''),
        ('Follow both barriers, not just the first capture', '''In the original replay White has a5, b5 and c5 against a7, b7 and c7, with both kings far away. The middle advance b6 invites ...axb6 or ...cxb6. After ...axb6, c6 draws the b7 pawn onto c6; a6 then runs on the cleared a-file. After ...cxb6, the mirror idea is a6, ...bxa6 and c6. Name which original pawn has left each barrier square. Visualizing only one capture can leave you pushing a pawn into a remaining blocker.'''),
        ('A check changes the race clock', '''Before the first push, count each side’s moves to promotion and include king moves, checks, captures and forced replies. The chapter’s Zubarev–Grigoriev example uses a king move away from an obvious recapture so that a later pawn capture will not give check. Saving that tempo changes who queens first. Our simpler replay isolates the deflection pattern; its ...c5 and ...c4 counter-race is illustrative. Other pawn advances or king approaches must be examined before making a broad forced-win claim about a new position.'''),
        ('Look for the defender’s refusal', '''An opponent need not capture every offered pawn. After b6, ask whether ...a6, ...c6, a king move, or a counter-break produces better resistance. If the pawn becomes passed but the king catches it, the sacrifice failed. If the opponent promotes with check before you can use your new queen, the final material count may mislead you. In your own games, first locate a possible breakthrough, then test acceptance on both sides and refusal. The board exercise asks only for the first move of this specified pattern, not a universal pawn-sacrifice rule.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why play c6 after b6 ...axb6?', 'To draw the b7 pawn away from the a-file barrier.', 'After ...bxc6, White’s a-pawn has an open route.', 'To protect the sacrificed pawn on b6.', 'c6 does not defend b6; its purpose is a second deflection.'),
        ('What must be counted besides pawn pushes?', 'Checks, captures, king moves and whether promotion comes with check.', 'Forcing moves change which player gets the next useful turn.', 'Only the number of pawns left after the first sacrifice.', 'A pawn deficit can be irrelevant if promotion arrives first, or fatal if the race was miscounted.'),
    ),
    ('Inspect both captures and the option to decline.', 'A passed pawn still needs enough time to reach promotion.'),
    ('Remove barriers deliberately.', 'Count the complete race.', 'Treat a checking promotion as a change in move order.'))

teach('zugzwang', (263, 264),
    ('Recognize the defensive duties that make a waiting move harmful.', 'Use an unequal bishop circuit to transfer the move.'),
    (
        ('The unwanted turn must have a reason', '''Zugzwang is a position in which the obligation to move worsens your situation. It is not just a cramped position or a feeling that every move looks unattractive. Identify what each legal move gives up. In diagram 372 Black’s bishop must help guard weak pawns on c6 and h5, and the king must prevent White’s entry through g5. White’s active formation can keep those targets under pressure. If Black could pass, its pieces could continue their jobs; chess supplies no passing move.'''),
        ('Why three squares beat two', '''White’s bishop can use f3, g2 and h1 while preserving the same useful diagonal. A direct f3–g2–f3 circuit uses two white moves and normally restores the old side to move. The three-move circuit f3–g2–h1–f3 changes the parity: the same formation returns with Black obliged to act. The displayed defence ...Bd7 and ...Be8 illustrates a losing choice of Black’s shorter bishop circuit; it is not claimed to be best resistance. Before pressing the replay arrow, predict which side will move when White returns to f3; counting this is the heart of the lesson.'''),
        ('Convert the concession instead of declaring victory', '''After the circuit, ...Bd7 permits Bxh5. A different bishop move, ...Bf7, releases c6 and permits Bxc6; after ...Bxc4, Be8 shows that the remaining targets and entry squares still matter. The book treats king moves that surrender g5 as losing, but deeper engine checks of the transcribed starting diagram instead find drawing king resources. Do not infer a forced win from this sample circuit. The replay follows one pawn-winning branch and ends after Be8. White still has a conversion task: restrict the bishop further and bring the king to the queenside or centre. Winning the first pawn is progress, not checkmate.'''),
        ('Transfer the method carefully', '''The next book example, Post–Leonhardt, first needs h4 and a bishop manoeuvre to induce ...h5, creating the extra weakness. Simply copying the short triangle gives Black a useful waiting reply. This is the limit of pattern learning: a spare pawn move, a longer defensive diagonal, a new exchange or an active king move can invalidate an apparent zugzwang. In diagram 372, Stockfish finds holds from the initial position even though the displayed bishop circuit loses; the lesson teaches the conditional tempo mechanism and preserves that source-versus-engine uncertainty. In your games list every piece’s duty and every reserve pawn move before choosing a waiting circuit. From the defender’s seat, look for a pawn tempo or a change of diagonal that preserves the key squares.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why does f3–g2–h1–f3 help?', 'It preserves the bishop’s duties while changing which side must move.', 'Three white bishop moves alter the parity of the returning formation.', 'Every three-move bishop route wins a pawn.', 'A circuit helps only when the opponent cannot make an equally useful waiting manoeuvre.'),
        ('What should the defender seek against zugzwang?', 'A useful spare tempo or a rearrangement that keeps the important duties.', 'An extra pawn move or diagonal can change the move-order balance.', 'Any legal move, without checking what it abandons.', 'The attacker wins precisely when a legal move must surrender a critical duty.'),
    ),
    ('Cramped does not automatically mean zugzwang.', 'A reserve pawn move can invalidate a memorized triangle.'),
    ('Name the defensive duties.', 'Count the waiting circuit.', 'Convert the specific weakness that is released.'))

teach('conversion', (264, 266),
    ('Prepare an outside passer before committing it.', 'Combine gradual improvement with a calculation checkpoint.'),
    (
        ('The passer is a threat you can keep in reserve', '''An outside passed pawn lies away from the main group of pawns and can draw the defender’s king or bishop to a distant wing. That diversion may let your king attack elsewhere. Its strength does not mean that every push is urgent. Advancing can give the opponent a convenient blockade square and a target that can no longer retreat. The chapter’s diagram 374 contrasts a premature b-pawn march with the more reliable plan of improving the bishop and centralizing the king first.'''),
        ('Improve with a concrete threat', '''Our original replay reduces the idea to a small inventory: White has Kd3, Bc2, b3 and g2; Black has Kf7, Bd6, e5 and g7. Ke4 both improves the king and attacks e5, asking Black to spend attention on defence. After the illustrative ...Ke6, Bd3 improves coordination before b4. No claim is made that this exact reduced position is a forced win. The exercise specifies the king improvement because the purpose is to learn what preparation does, not guess an engine’s top move.'''),
        ('No haste does not mean no calculation', '''If the opponent threatens promotion, a forcing check or an exchange of your last pawn, a quiet improvement may be too slow. Before postponing a pawn push, identify the opponent’s fastest counterplay. Before finally pushing, trace the likely blockade and check whether your bishop and king can remove it. Botvinnik–Thomas illustrates keeping an extra knight rather than voluntarily entering a delicate pawn ending: the apparently leisurely king route was a practical way to force the defender’s pawn to move and fall.'''),
        ('Three routes to conversion can cooperate', '''The chapter organizes conversion through positional pressure, combinations and simplification. They are tools that can appear in one ending. Pressure ties a defender to a weakness; a tactic displaces it; an exchange reaches a known winning king-and-pawn ending. Diagram 376 demonstrates a bishop manoeuvre and pawn sacrifice that eliminate a blockade when routine pushing fails. Use a two-stage explanation in your games: first name the improvement or restriction you want, then calculate the exact move sequence that achieves it. Reassess when the defender chooses a different plan.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why may an outside pawn wait?', 'Keeping it back can preserve options while king and bishop improve.', 'Preparation can prevent a convenient blockade and strengthen the eventual diversion.', 'A passed pawn loses strength every turn it stays still.', 'Its value depends on the whole position, not an automatic obligation to push.'),
        ('When is a quiet improvement too slow?', 'When the opponent has an urgent forcing threat such as promotion or a decisive check.', 'Immediate threats set the calculation deadline.', 'Whenever your material count is larger.', 'A material lead does not tell you whether time is available.'),
    ),
    ('An irreversible push can give the defender its ideal square.', 'A methodical plan still needs a concrete threat check.'),
    ('Improve the escort.', 'Restrict the blockader.', 'Push when the resulting position, not impatience, justifies it.'))

teach('exchange-advantage', (267, 287),
    ('Explain what a rook can do that a bishop cannot.', 'Recognize the limits of the exchange advantage without useful pawns.'),
    (
        ('An advantage of movement, not a guaranteed result', '''Having a rook against a bishop or knight is called being the exchange up. The rook can change files, ranks and square colours, while a bishop is limited to diagonals of one colour. With pawns on both wings, this range can create entry points and win targets the bishop cannot cover together. Without pawns, rook against a minor piece is commonly drawn with sound defence. Before trading into the ending, inspect actual lines, pawn threats and king exposure rather than relying only on the usual point values.'''),
        ('Open the lines that make the rook useful', '''The Levenfish–Freiman example first arranges an entry file, wins a queenside pawn, then attacks kingside weaknesses and creates a passer. The method is to make the rook invade, not shuffle on its own back rank. Our replay uses the later theoretical diagram 414 to isolate another use of lines: White has Kg4, Ra6 and an h-pawn on h5 against Black’s Kh7 and Be3. The black bishop and h8 corner are dark, so White can use the opposite-coloured access squares to restrict the king.'''),
        ('The pawn can obstruct the stronger side', '''After Kf5 and Rg6, h6 offers a pawn that is partly shielding the black king from h-file checks. Rg7+ then drives the king onto h6 in the displayed branch, and Rg6+ forces it back near the edge. Do not summarize this as “give away a pawn because the rook wins.” The king and rook had to take away escape routes first. Other bishop replies require their own calculation; the replay teaches the restriction and opening of lines, stopping before the longer theoretical conversion.'''),
        ('Respect the defender’s compensation', '''A bishop accompanied by dangerous passed pawns may draw or even beat a rook. The chapter’s Verlinsky–Maizelis ending shows two connected passers outrunning a rook’s attempt to collect another pawn. Bernstein–Rubinstein shows that one king tempo and active rook placement can determine whether a passed pawn breaks through. When defending the exchange down, keep the bishop active, unite it with the king where useful, and create a promotion threat that gives the rook a defensive job. A passive minor piece with no targets is much easier to overcome.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('What is the normal rook plan with useful pawns?', 'Open or occupy lines, invade, attack weaknesses and create a passer.', 'The rook’s greater range matters when it can reach real targets.', 'Exchange every pawn immediately.', 'Removing all pawns can leave a drawable rook-versus-minor ending.'),
        ('Why is h6 offered only after improving king and rook?', 'The sacrifice must expose a restricted king rather than let it escape.', 'The barrier and approaching king make the opened lines meaningful.', 'Every rook wins against a bishop without pawns.', 'Well-placed king and minor piece often hold the pawnless ending.'),
    ),
    ('Do not remove the pawns that supply your winning chances without checking the result.', 'Enemy passers may be worth more than the nominal exchange.'),
    ('Make the rook’s range matter.', 'Restrict before sacrificing.', 'Calculate the defender’s promotion threats.'))

teach('rook-behind', (268, 271),
    ('Explain the geometry behind “rooks behind passed pawns”.', 'Recognize when active rank defence is a better temporary job.'),
    (
        ('Support that grows as the pawn moves', '''A rook behind its own passed pawn continues to defend it along the file after each advance. The pawn moves away from the rook, opening more squares between them; it does not run into its protector. A rook placed in front may eventually block the promotion square and need a costly manoeuvre to get out. Behind an enemy pawn, the same geometry gives the defending rook more checking room as the pawn advances. The rule describes a useful arrangement, but immediate checks, captures and king shelters still take priority.'''),
        ('Inspect both rooks in the replay', '''White has Kg2, Ra4 and a b5 passer, while Black has Kg7, Rh8 and h7. Rb4 places the white rook behind b5. After the illustrative ...Kf6 and b6, Black chooses ...Rb8 to blockade from the front. White can play b7 with the pawn still defended by Rb4, but cannot simply promote through a black rook on b8. Pause there and name the unfinished job: dislodge or exchange the blockader without losing the pawn. Good placement supports progress; it does not complete every conversion by itself.'''),
        ('The active rook can change its job', '''In Botvinnik–Boleslavsky the rook first goes behind its own outside b-pawn, using the pawn to tie the defender down. Later it moves across to defend the pawn from the sixth rank, gaining more activity against the other wing. The point is not loyalty to one file. Once a new arrangement keeps the pawn safe and creates an entry or checking threat, a rank defence can be stronger. Each rook move should be explained by the duties it keeps and the new target it reaches.'''),
        ('Defend actively and check the exchange', '''When your rook is behind the opponent’s pawn, look for checks against an approaching king and attacks on a second pawn. A rook frozen in front may lack those resources. Before exchanging rooks, remove them mentally and ask whether your king can stop the resulting pawn ending. The chapter’s long practical example ends by building a bridge, joining the rear-rook principle to a precise theoretical win. In your own games, use the rule to generate candidate squares, then compare checking distance, king access, promotion control and tactical safety.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why does rear support continue after b5–b6?', 'The pawn opens the file between itself and the supporting rook.', 'The rook’s line of defence lengthens as the pawn advances.', 'The rook starts moving like a bishop.', 'The geometry remains a file, not a diagonal.'),
        ('Must a rook stay behind its own passer forever?', 'No; a safe rank defence may activate it against another target.', 'Reassign the rook when the pawn remains secure and the new activity is concrete.', 'Yes; every sideways move violates the rule.', 'The rule explains a common good arrangement, not an absolute movement restriction.'),
    ),
    ('A pawn on the seventh cannot promote through an occupied promotion square.', 'Before a rook exchange, evaluate the remaining king-and-pawn ending.'),
    ('Start with rear support.', 'Keep useful checking distance.', 'Change jobs when the pawn stays safe and a real target appears.'))

teach('defensive-exchanges', (271, 273),
    ('Identify the material the stronger side needs for winning chances.', 'Seek a useful last-pawn exchange from the defending side.'),
    (
        ('Defence has a destination too', '''An endgame defender should seek a specific drawable material arrangement. King and lone bishop cannot checkmate a bare king; neither can king and lone knight. Removing the last pawns can therefore be more valuable than protecting an apparently harmless pawn. A defender may willingly give up its final pawn if the exchange also removes the attacker’s last pawn. This is a result calculation, not a material-counting mistake. The attacker must recognize the same destination and avoid entering it when a winning alternative remains.'''),
        ('Account for every remaining pawn', '''Our original illustration has White Kh1, Nf2 and g4 against Black Kf5 and h5, with Black to move. The white pawn checks the black king on f5, so Black must answer legally. ...hxg4 both removes the checker and offers Black’s pawn on g4. If White accepts with Nxg4, every pawn is gone and only king and knight remain against king. The game is immediately drawn by insufficient material. White is not forced to accept; the replay shows the defensive target, and the exercise specifically asks for the checking pawn’s capture.'''),
        ('A piece advantage may need preservation, not simplification', '''The book’s Nimzowitsch–Rubinstein ending is harder: White has an extra knight but Black can arrange pawn exchanges with ...f6 and ...e5–e4. Careless knight play allows the last white pawn to disappear. Better king placement and a knight post on d7 prevent the critical exchange, retaining winning chances. This shows why “trade when ahead” needs a condition. Trade hostile activity when the resulting ending is good; do not automatically trade away the pawn that lets your extra minor piece create a promotion threat.'''),
        ('Other defensive destinations', '''Opposite-coloured bishops can also offer blockade chances even when several pawns remain. Stalemate, repeated checks and an active rook can supply different drawing routes. Select the destination that fits the actual pieces, then calculate a legal path into it. Two knights against a bare king cannot normally force mate, but positions with an enemy pawn can be special because the pawn supplies waiting moves; do not treat every two-knight ending as identical. For beginner practice, start by asking after every exchange: can either remaining army possibly deliver checkmate?'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why is ...hxg4 a defensive resource?', 'It answers the pawn check and offers an exchange removing both final pawns.', 'After Nxg4 the remaining knight cannot mate a bare king.', 'It wins White’s knight immediately.', 'The knight remains on the board; the resource is the drawable material ending.'),
        ('Should the stronger side always exchange pawns?', 'No; the final pawn may be essential to make the extra minor piece useful.', 'Evaluate the material and promotion chances after the exchange.', 'Yes; fewer pawns always make conversion easier.', 'Fewer pawns can leave insufficient material or a theoretical blockade.'),
    ),
    ('Verify that an exchange answers any current check.', 'Do not describe an optional accepting capture as forced.'),
    ('Name a drawable destination.', 'Count every surviving pawn.', 'Preserve the pawn that supplies the winning threat.'))

teach('opposition', (273, 275),
    ('Understand opposition as control of entry squares.', 'Follow a leading king through to a protected promotion.'),
    (
        ('Kings create a barrier without touching', '''Kings may never occupy adjacent squares. With kings facing each other and one square between them, each prevents the other from moving directly forward. If one side must move and has no helpful pawn tempo, it may have to give way and allow an entry square. This relationship is called opposition. Its purpose is penetration or denial of penetration. Do not take opposition merely to use the term: locate the pawn, its promotion square and the king route that the desired opposition will make available.'''),
        ('Get the king in front', '''In the original replay White Ke6 leads its e5 pawn against Black Ke8. White first chooses Kd6, retaining useful access to the seventh rank; against the displayed ...Kf7 it advances e6+ and then e7 after ...Ke8. Before the final push, Kd7 protects e8. This last detail prevents Black from simply capturing the new queen. The black moves are one legal defensive branch, not the only choices. Reset after Kd6 and investigate ...Kd8 as well: the useful lesson is the leading king’s access, not memorization of a single king retreat.'''),
        ('Pawn tempi and the side to move belong in the position', '''A diagram without its side to move is incomplete. In diagram 386 the unadvanced queenside pawns let White arrange the final pawn move and compel the opposing king to yield. Moving a pawn one square rather than two can preserve a future tempo. Conversely, a pawn move may release the opponent’s blockade. Before using opposition, list spare pawn moves for both sides and determine whether the king who seems obliged to retreat can make one instead. Distant opposition is useful only if it can be converted into the needed near opposition.'''),
        ('Rook pawns and blockade exceptions', '''A rook pawn has only one neighbouring file, so the usual king-outflanking route can disappear at the edge. A king trapped beside its own pawn can also create stalemate. The chapter’s adjacent pawn examples show that an extra pawn is not a substitute for space. Test the final move explicitly: is the opponent checked, can its king move, and is the promoted piece protected? In your games calculate at least to the point where the pawn’s promotion is safe or the defender has a clear hold. “I have opposition” is a reason to investigate, not the end of the calculation.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('What is opposition for?', 'Winning or denying a king entry square.', 'The relationship matters because it changes access to the pawn or promotion area.', 'Collecting a positional label regardless of pawn location.', 'An opposition position can be useless if it does not help the concrete task.'),
        ('Why does Kd7 come before e8=Q in the replay?', 'It protects the promotion square from the defending king.', 'A new queen must not be freely capturable.', 'The pawn cannot promote until its king is on rank seven.', 'There is no such rule; king support matters only when the resulting square needs protection.'),
    ),
    ('Always include the side to move and spare pawn tempi.', 'Do not transplant a central-pawn rule to a corner without checking the room.'),
    ('Lead with the king when possible.', 'Use opposition to gain access.', 'Protect the actual promotion square.'))

teach('pawn-tempi', (276, 277),
    ('Count waiting moves before committing pawn advances.', 'Explain why promotion threats with and without check differ.'),
    (
        ('A pawn move can be a clock setting', '''In diagram 393 White has Kf5 with g3 and h4 against Ke7 and h7. The king wants h6, the pawns want g5 and h5, and the defender will oscillate near g8 and h8. There is no spare piece to make a harmless waiting move. A pawn’s one-square or two-square first advance therefore changes the future side to move permanently. Treat that choice as part of the calculation, not a matter of convenience. Once the pawn has advanced, the missed tempo cannot be recreated by retreating it.'''),
        ('Find the critical future position first', '''White wins in the intended formation when g5–g6 occurs with the black king on h8. After ...Kg8, g6–g7 does not check the king on g8: the pawn attacks f8 and h8. Black must then leave g8, allowing the white king to capture h7 while protecting the passer. If the same g7 advance gives check to a king on h8, ...Kg8 can instead put the defender on the correct square with the correct move order. The checking status of one pawn push changes the whole result.'''),
        ('Count back to the move you control', '''The replay begins Kg5, ...Kf7, Kh6, ...Kg8. Then h5 and the two separate advances g4 and g5 arrange the desired king square before g6. Pause at every pawn push and say where Black’s king will be after its reply. The chapter calls this a colour rule: in this particular two-pawn formation, compare the kings’ square colours and choose the pawn parity accordingly. Use the colour observation as a cross-check on your move count, not a universal rule for every pawn ending or an excuse to omit king alternatives.'''),
        ('How the defender resists', '''Black wants the critical pawn advance to occur on the wrong turn, or wants to force White’s king away from h6. In a different starting arrangement, Black may have a useful pawn move or king route that breaks the oscillation. Recalculate rather than copying h5, g4 and g5 mechanically. In a real game, first identify the final position you need, then count the available moves backward. This is the same discipline used in triangulation, but here the irreversible pawn moves supply the spare tempo. Explain which exact move you are saving and what future reply it changes.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Does a white pawn on g7 check a black king on g8?', 'No; it attacks f8 and h8.', 'Pawn attacks are diagonal, so g8 is not attacked by a pawn directly below it.', 'Yes; every seventh-rank pawn checks the square ahead.', 'Pawns move forward but capture and attack diagonally.'),
        ('Why can a one-square advance be better than a two-square advance?', 'It can preserve an extra waiting move needed for the final king geometry.', 'The future side to move can decide whether the critical pawn push gives check.', 'A two-square first move is illegal in an endgame.', 'The usual pawn rule still applies if both squares are clear.'),
    ),
    ('Do not confuse the pawn’s forward move with its attacked squares.', 'The colour rule belongs to this specific formation, not all endings.'),
    ('Locate the desired final turn.', 'Count pawn tempi backward.', 'Check whether the critical advance gives check.'))

teach('pawn-draws', (274, 276),
    ('Recognize a corner blockade that survives an extra pawn.', 'Distinguish legal king pressure from an actual promotion route.'),
    (
        ('Extra material can run out of useful moves', '''Diagram 389 has White Kc4, a5 and b6 against Black Kb7 and a6. White is a pawn ahead, but the a-pawns lock each other and the b-pawn has only b7 and b8 left. Black can retreat toward the corner and use the edge to prevent an outflanking king route. Count useful moves rather than only units: White’s a5 pawn cannot advance, so in effect the whole winning attempt depends on whether the king can make the b-pawn’s promotion safe.'''),
        ('Why the apparent progress ends in stalemate', '''The replay follows Kc5, ...Kb8, Kc6, ...Kc8, b7+, ...Kb8 and Kb6. White now controls a7 and c7 with its king; the b7 pawn controls a8 and c8. Black’s king on b8 has no square, and the a6 pawn cannot move through the white pawn on a5. However, b8 is not attacked. With Black to move the game is stalemate. White has surrounded the defender so completely that it has removed the compulsory king move it needed.'''),
        ('A shifted position is a new position', '''The book then shifts the arrangement down a rank or toward the centre and obtains a win. That change creates manoeuvring room for the kings before the pawn runs out of moves. Opposition can then force the defender away instead of freezing it in a corner. This is why copying only the relative distance between the kings is unsafe: the edge is an active feature of the position. Before declaring that a known draw applies, compare the pawn’s file, its remaining advances, both kings and the side to move.'''),
        ('Practical defensive discipline', '''When defending an edge blockade, stay in the shelter and preserve the arrangement that leaves the attacker no useful breakthrough. A greedy pawn capture can release the blocked pawn or give the attacking king an entry. When attacking, look for a distant reserve tempo, a pawn break or a route that avoids stalemate; if none exists, accept that the material lead is not convertible. The exercise asks for the sample approach so you can observe its limit. It does not label Kc5 a winning move or pretend that the final stalemate was an accidental rules curiosity.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why is the replay’s final position drawn?', 'Black has no legal move and is not in check.', 'The king is confined and the a-pawn is blocked, producing stalemate.', 'The b-pawn has reached rank seven, where pawns cannot promote.', 'Promotion is possible on rank eight; the issue is that the game ends before another move.'),
        ('Why can shifting a formation away from the edge change the result?', 'It can create room for an outflanking king manoeuvre.', 'The board boundary limits the useful squares and waiting moves.', 'All translated positions have the same result.', 'Relative piece distances do not preserve the relationship to the edge.'),
    ),
    ('Surrounding the king without check can end the game as a draw.', 'Do not translate an edge position without re-evaluating the available squares.'),
    ('Count useful pawn moves.', 'Inspect the corner shelter.', 'Check stalemate before celebrating the blockade.'))

teach('same-bishops', (277, 278),
    ('Coordinate king and bishop against a promotion blockade.', 'Explain why a short defensive diagonal can be a liability.'),
    (
        ('Both bishops can contest the same squares', '''With bishops on the same colour, the attacker can exchange or drive away the bishop that guards the pawn’s route. A lone extra pawn still does not guarantee success: the defender’s king may occupy the promotion square and its bishop may have a safe long diagonal. Diagram 394 gives White Ka6, Ba4 and b6 against Kc8 and Bd5, all bishops on light squares. White must control the king’s shelter and then remove the bishop’s access to b7; pushing alone does not solve those two different jobs.'''),
        ('The king takes away the shelter first', '''Ka7 is played before the bishop manoeuvre, preventing ...Kb8 from settling in front of the passer. Bb5 and Ba6+ then drive the king from c8. Only after that does Bb7 challenge the critical diagonal. Notice how the pieces cooperate: the king supports the near-promotion area while the bishop supplies long-range checks and exchanges. Before each arrow, name whether the next move improves the king restriction, gives a forcing check, or changes the bishop’s defensive diagonal. Several quiet moves are purposeful preparation for one concrete release.'''),
        ('Attack the diagonal, not the bishop’s current square alone', '''After ...Be6, Bf3 and ...Bc8, Bg4 attacks the bishop through f5, e6 and d7. The defender’s short c8–b7–a6 diagonal gives it little room to keep controlling b7 without being exchanged or driven away. The related diagram 395 explains why vertical king opposition can improve the defence and why a central pawn’s longer diagonal may hold where a flank pawn fails. The precise bishop route matters: an arbitrary bishop retreat can give the defender the waiting square it lacked.'''),
        ('What the defender should seek', '''Keep the king in front of the passer, choose a diagonal with enough safe retreat squares, and avoid an exchange that reaches a lost pawn ending. Do not trade bishops simply because that removes the attacker’s bishop; remove both bishops mentally and count the kings and pawn afterward. For the attacker, the correct question is “which square must be denied to make the blockader move?” In your games, inventory the promotion square, the king shelter and the bishop diagonal as three separate items before deciding whether your extra pawn supplies a realistic winning method.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('What is Ka7 preventing?', 'The defending king’s simple occupation of b8.', 'The promotion shelter must be denied before the bishops can clear the route.', 'A black pawn advance on the a-file.', 'There is no black pawn in this diagram; the key defender is the king.'),
        ('Why can a short blocking diagonal be bad for the defender?', 'The bishop may run out of safe squares while retaining the blockade.', 'King restriction and a bishop attack can exhaust its waiting room.', 'Bishops may only move three squares in an ending.', 'A bishop has no fixed distance limit; obstacles and attacked squares restrict it.'),
    ),
    ('A bishop exchange is good only if the resulting pawn ending is good.', 'A central pawn may give the defender a longer diagonal than a flank pawn.'),
    ('Deny the king shelter.', 'Contest the blocking diagonal.', 'Count the defender’s safe retreat squares.'))

teach('opposite-bishops', (278, 279),
    ('Set up or break the typical opposite-bishop pawn blockade.', 'Advance the pawn whose destination can be supported by the king.'),
    (
        ('The defender can cover a colour the attacker cannot reach', '''Opposite-coloured bishops live on different square colours and can never exchange each other directly. A defending bishop can therefore block a pawn on its own colour without being attacked by the other bishop. The defending king handles another pawn or entry square. This division of duties often draws one or even two extra pawns. It is a common defensive resource, especially with connected pawns not yet far enough advanced. It is not a rule that every opposite-bishop ending is drawn: king access and pawn spacing still matter.'''),
        ('Identify the actual blockade', '''In diagram 396 White Ke5, Ba6, e6 and f6 face Black Ke8 and Bb4. The bishop on b4 attacks e7, so pushing e7 immediately loses that pawn if it is not king-supported. Pushing f7+ first instead lets the defender simplify and establish a hold. Bb5+ moves the defending king before White selects the king route. After ...Kf8, Kd5–c6–d7 brings the king to e7’s support; if the king went the other way, White would use the corresponding route toward f7.'''),
        ('Protect the advanced pawn with a piece that can reach it', '''White’s light bishop cannot defend the dark e7 square. The f6 pawn can recapture a bishop there, but the resulting e7 pawn needs king protection. Once Kd7 has arrived, e7+ Bxe7 fxe7+ leaves that new pawn protected. White must not play Kxe7, which would place its king adjacent to Black’s king on f8. The replay’s bishop waiting moves are illustrative legal resistance preserving the diagonal, not a claim that Black has only one legal move each turn. Pause before e7 and verify the bishop’s colour, the f-pawn recapture and the king’s protection of the resulting pawn. The method works because the king has actually reached the correct support square.'''),
        ('The basic defensive formation and its limits', '''Diagram 397 has White e5/f5, king f4 and bishop d4 against king f7 and bishop c8. Moving the white king to support e6 can abandon f5; the defender divides its tasks successfully. Diagram 398 then exploits a badly restricted bishop with king manoeuvres and zugzwang before pushing. In a real game compare your formation with both patterns. The defender wants a stable king-and-bishop division of labour; the attacker wants an advanced supported pawn, a second distant target or a way to deprive the bishop of safe waiting moves.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why must the king support e7?', 'White’s light bishop cannot protect that dark square.', 'A bishop never changes its square colour, so the king must supply the missing protection.', 'The king is required by the rules to stand beside every promoting pawn.', 'That is not a rule; here it is a concrete response to ...Bxe7.'),
        ('Are opposite-coloured bishop endings automatically drawn?', 'No; advanced supported pawns, king access or restricted defence can win.', 'The defensive tendency has precise geometric exceptions.', 'Yes, regardless of pawn location.', 'The chapter’s winning examples show why that shortcut fails.'),
    ),
    ('Do not push the wrong connected pawn into a known blockade.', 'Different bishop colours make king support especially important.'),
    ('Name each defender’s duty.', 'Support the critical advance.', 'Break the formation before pushing into it.'))

teach('separated-passers', (279, 280),
    ('Use distant passed pawns to stretch the defending bishop.', 'Keep one passer safe while the king helps the other.'),
    (
        ('Distance can defeat one diagonal', '''Two connected pawns may both be held by one bishop diagonal while the king blocks their escort. Separated pawns can force the defender to cover distant promotion areas instead. In diagram 400 White has Kd5, Bh5 and c6/f5 against Kc7 and Be7. There are two intervening files between c and f. Black’s king is occupied with c6 while the bishop must contain the f-pawn. White can exploit that division only if it does not let the c-pawn fall as soon as the king walks away.'''),
        ('Arrange the replacement guard first', '''Bf3 creates a long diagonal toward c6. Initially Kd5 blocks that diagonal, but once the king leaves for e6 the bishop’s path f3–e4–d5–c6 becomes clear. This is a useful visualization checkpoint: remove the king from d5 mentally and trace the entire diagonal before moving it. The king can then go to the f-pawn, protecting its advances while the bishop takes over the old guarding duty. A good king journey does not abandon one pawn; it transfers the job to another piece.'''),
        ('Make the king challenge the blockader', '''Ke6, f6 and f7 bring the king and pawn forward together. Black’s bishop moves through b4 and a3, retaining lines toward the promotion area. Kf6 and Kg6 then approach g7, where White can support f8 and deprive the bishop of a comfortable blockade. The checking try ...Bc3+ gains a tempo but does not restore the lost king access. The replay ends at Kg7, the source’s decisive coordination point, rather than playing an artificial sequence of cooperative moves to promotion.'''),
        ('Do not turn distance into an absolute formula', '''The adjacent source diagram with only one intervening file illustrates a hold, but the number of files is a clue rather than a universal result. Advanced pawns, bishops controlling promotion squares, exposed kings or a tactical sacrifice can change the answer. The defender should seek a diagonal that watches both pawns, force the attacker’s king away with checks, or sacrifice the bishop only when both pawns are stopped. The attacker should preserve both threats until one defender is overloaded. Test the exact position after each king move, especially any pawn that was previously defended only by that king.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('What changes when White’s king leaves d5?', 'Bf3’s diagonal opens and the bishop takes over protection of c6.', 'The king’s departure removes a friendly blocker from f3–c6.', 'The c-pawn no longer needs protection.', 'Black’s king remains near c6, so abandoning the pawn would destroy the two-wing plan.'),
        ('What is White’s king trying to do near g7?', 'Support promotion and challenge the bishop holding the f-pawn.', 'The king helps the pawn that the distant defending king cannot reach.', 'Chase Black’s king on the c-file.', 'The whole point is to let the c-pawn tie that king down while play succeeds elsewhere.'),
    ),
    ('Transfer a pawn’s defensive duty before its king leaves.', 'Pawn separation alone is not a complete win test.'),
    ('Keep both threats alive.', 'Use the bishop as the distant guard.', 'Bring the king to the pawn held by the bishop.'))

teach('knight-blockade', (280, 282),
    ('Find a knight route that covers two pawn advances.', 'Notice how edge geometry removes defensive knight routes.'),
    (
        ('A knight needs route squares, not a long diagonal', '''A bishop can restrain distant pawns from an open diagonal; a knight must reach a local square with the right attacks. This makes a knight vulnerable to very advanced or widely separated passers, especially a rook pawn with no board on its outer side. Yet a well-timed double attack can stop two connected pawns. Diagram 402 has White Kh7 and Ne1 against Black Kf8 with pawns g4 and h4. The king is distant, so the knight must first stabilize the pawns by itself.'''),
        ('Build the route backward from the double attack', '''Nf2 attacks both g4 and h3. White therefore starts Nd3, preparing that useful square. If Black pushes ...h3, Nf2 attacks both pawns at once, and after ...h2 White plays Nh1. The knight on h1 occupies the promotion square and controls g3, so neither pawn can advance safely. If Black instead chooses ...g3 after Nd3, the route is Nf4, ...g2 and Nh3, with the same purpose of combining blockade and capture threats. Read pawn attacks carefully: a black pawn on h2 attacks g1, not h1.'''),
        ('Blocking buys time for the king', '''The knight’s task is not necessarily to capture a pawn immediately. A stable blockade gives White time to bring the king over while the opponent cannot make useful pawn progress. Do not leave the blockade just to give an unrelated check. In diagram 405 a precise checking route Nd2+, Nf3+ and Nh2 draws against an advanced f-pawn, while the tempting Ne3+ leads the king onto more useful squares. A forcing check is good only if the resulting king route serves your defensive aim.'''),
        ('Why a one-file shift can lose', '''Move the same scheme toward the edge and the knight may need a square outside the board. The chapter explicitly contrasts a shifted advanced-pawn position where the missing equivalent of h2 prevents the defensive circuit. Diagram 401 shows a rook pawn that outruns a knight with no usable outer route. Before claiming a blockade, list the knight’s next two destinations and check that the king cannot capture or exclude it. From the attacking side, push only after calculating the knight fork or capture that the next square makes possible.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('What does Nf2 attack after ...h3?', 'Both g4 and h3.', 'Those are two of the knight’s actual jump destinations from f2.', 'The promotion squares g1 and h1.', 'A knight on f2 does not attack either of those squares.'),
        ('Why can the same pattern fail nearer the edge?', 'An essential knight route square may fall outside the board.', 'A knight needs actual legal intermediate posts, not just a diagram’s visual resemblance.', 'Knights become unable to give check on edge files.', 'Knights can check near an edge; their available routes are simply fewer.'),
    ),
    ('Do not abandon a stable blockade for a purposeless check.', 'An advanced rook pawn can exploit the knight’s missing outer route.'),
    ('Work backward from a double attack.', 'Block before collecting.', 'Verify the next two knight squares.'))

teach('minor-duel', (281, 283),
    ('Use a knight’s checking tempo to protect a pawn while winning a bishop.', 'Compare a bishop’s waiting moves with a knight’s fixed colour change.'),
    (
        ('The single pawn makes piece placement precise', '''With bishop and pawn against knight, or knight and pawn against bishop, an extra pawn often cannot promote if the defending king controls its destination. The stronger side needs an exception: an excluded king, an effective blockade-breaking manoeuvre or a tactic that wins the minor piece. Do not assume that a bishop is always stronger in a pawn ending, or that a knight always wins because it can reach both colours. Identify the promotion square and the job each king is doing before comparing the pieces.'''),
        ('Keep the pawn while the king takes the bishop', '''Diagram 407 gives White Kf7, Nf4 and g5 against Kc6 and Bh8. White’s king can approach h8, but Black’s king heads toward the pawn. Nh5 and Ng7 prepare Ne6+, a checking jump that also protects g5. The king can therefore play Kg8 and later Kxh8 without simply leaving its pawn behind. Pause after ...Kf4: what captures might Black threaten, and which knight move both checks and changes the pawn’s protection? The answer comes from exact squares, not the piece-value scale.'''),
        ('Different colours are a resource with a cost', '''Every knight move changes its square colour. A bishop remains on the same colour and may have several waiting squares along a safe diagonal. In the related diagram 406, the bishop changes diagonals and gains a tempo while always controlling f6, preventing a knight checking route. In our replay the knight uses its colour-changing route to combine defence and check. Each piece has a distinct timing ability: a bishop can often wait while retaining a line, but a knight cannot stay on the same-coloured post in one move.'''),
        ('Calculate after winning the minor piece', '''Kxh8 removes the bishop, but White must still keep its pawn and convert the resulting knight-and-pawn ending. Here the knight on e6 guards g5, which is why the capture was timed correctly. A careless king capture in a different arrangement might permit ...Kxg5 and leave king and knight against king, a draw. When reviewing your own games, continue the calculation through the opponent’s reply after a material win. Ask what guards the last pawn now and whether the defending king can force its exchange. The opponent’s best resistance is often a pawn capture rather than saving the attacked piece.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why is Ne6+ useful after ...Kf4?', 'It checks while controlling g5, buying time for Kxh8.', 'One move supplies both the forcing tempo and the pawn’s replacement guard.', 'It is a double check by two white pieces.', 'Only the knight checks in this position; no discovered line checker has been established.'),
        ('Why must the last pawn still be checked after Kxh8?', 'Without it, the remaining knight cannot mate a bare king.', 'The conversion depends on keeping a promotion threat.', 'Winning a bishop ends the game immediately.', 'Only checkmate or another ending condition ends the game, not a minor-piece capture.'),
    ),
    ('A knight move always changes square colour.', 'Do not win a bishop by accidentally giving away the final winning pawn.'),
    ('Coordinate the king route and pawn guard.', 'Use a check for a concrete tempo.', 'Re-evaluate the remaining material.'))

teach('rook-v-pawn', (283, 284),
    ('Avoid checks that escort the pawn’s king forward.', 'Recognize the checking underpromotion that saves a tempo.'),
    (
        ('More material still needs a stopping method', '''A rook against a pawn usually wins if the pawn is not advanced and the rook’s king can approach. The rook can cut the enemy king off, attack the pawn from the side, or occupy the promotion file. Repeated checks are not automatically progress. If every check lets the defending king step closer to the pawn’s destination, the rook may be supplying a free escort. Count the pawn’s next move and the king’s useful reply before checking, especially when your own king is still several moves away.'''),
        ('One rank changes the result', '''The book’s diagram 408 has White Ke8 and Rh7 against Kd6 and c6; careful rook manoeuvres win. The replay uses the book’s explicitly shifted case: Ke7 and Rh6 against Kd5 and c5. That one rank gives Black enough time. Rh5+ is met by ...Kd4, and further checks lead the king down through d3, d2 and d1 while the pawn advances. The white king approaches too, but it never establishes the promotion blockade before the black king supports c1.'''),
        ('Underpromotion is about the next turn', '''After the replay’s ...c2, Rh2+ and ...Kd1, White plays Kd3. Black now promotes to a knight with check: c1=N+ attacks d3. A queen on c1 would be more powerful in general but would not check the king on d3, allowing Rh1 checkmate: the rook reaches h1 while Kd3 covers the king’s escapes on c2, d2 and e2, and the new queen on c1 blocks another escape. The knight’s forcing check prevents that immediate mate and gains the tempo to reach the rook-versus-knight defensive family. The exercise asks for the checking promotion, so examine all promotion pieces rather than automatically selecting a queen.'''),
        ('Attack the dangerous pawn, not the convenient one', '''The next source example, diagram 409, adds a remote a-pawn to a protected g-pawn. White must first contain the king-supported pawn with king opposition and timely checks; taking the distant a-pawn immediately wastes the winning tempo. This is a transferable race habit: identify which pawn is actually urgent and which can be caught later. From the defending side, keep the king near the advanced pawn and look for a checking promotion. From the rook side, prioritize promotion control and useful king access over a long sequence of checks that changes nothing in your favour.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why choose c1=N+ instead of an automatic queen?', 'The knight checks Kd3 and gains a forcing tempo.', 'The new piece’s immediate attack, not only its usual value, decides the move.', 'A queen cannot promote on the c-file.', 'Any pawn can promote to queen, rook, bishop or knight on its last rank.'),
        ('What can be wrong with repeated rook checks?', 'They may guide the opposing king toward a useful escort square.', 'A check must be judged by the reply it permits.', 'Checks are illegal when the rook side has no pawns.', 'The legality of checking has nothing to do with having pawns.'),
    ),
    ('Count the resulting king route after every check.', 'The replay is the shifted drawing case, not the original winning diagram.'),
    ('Control promotion.', 'Use the king with the rook.', 'Check every promotion choice for a forcing tempo.'))

teach('connected-v-rook', (284, 285),
    ('Coordinate two connected passers and their king against a rook.', 'Explain why the inner pawn’s lead changes the rook’s counterplay.'),
    (
        ('Pawns near promotion can outweigh a rook', '''Connected passed pawns defend one another’s forward capture squares and can create threats too quickly for a rook to stop both. Their strength depends on rank, king support and the opposing king’s distance. Two pawns still on their starting squares are not equivalent to two supported sixth-rank passers. Diagram 410 has White Kg3, g4 and h5 against Black Kb4 and Ra2. Black’s king is remote, but the rook can check, attack from behind or sacrifice for a pawn. White must prevent those resources from working together.'''),
        ('Why the inner pawn leads', '''White plays g5 and then g6 rather than rushing h6. The g-pawn’s route lies closer to White’s king, while the outer h-pawn stays farther from Black’s approaching king. If Black attacks h5 from the side, the leading g-pawn can threaten promotion before the rook has time to stop it. Pawn order is therefore a concrete geometric choice. In the replay ...Ra1 and ...Kc5 are plausible defensive manoeuvres; White’s Kf4 prepares to support the leading pawn and limit the hostile king.'''),
        ('The king route does two jobs', '''After ...Rg1, Ke5 heads toward f6 while also denying Black’s useful approach squares d5 and d6. White is not simply running toward its own pawn: it is restricting the other king at the same time. Kf6 then supports g7. When g7 arrives, ...Rxg7 is met by the king, leaving the remaining pawn able to continue. Before advancing, verify the exact support square; a king one rank farther back may fail to protect the pawn at the moment the rook captures it.'''),
        ('The defender’s checks may change the pawn order', '''The chapter compares altered white king squares and shows that a timely intermediate rook check can force the outer pawn to advance first, producing a drawn formation. That is why even a king apparently nearer the pawns can have the wrong checking exposure. From Black’s seat look for a check before attacking a pawn, then seek a rear position against the leading pawn. From White’s seat test those checks before pushing. The replay demonstrates a winning source method from its exact starting arrangement; it does not assert that every pair of connected passers beats a rook.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why prefer g6 to an early h6 in this formation?', 'The inner pawn can lead with king support while the outer pawn remains farther from the enemy king.', 'The pawn order changes which pawn the rook and distant king can attack.', 'Rook pawns are forbidden to advance before knight pawns.', 'There is no such rule; the exact geometry makes the difference.'),
        ('What extra job does Ke5 perform?', 'It restricts the approaching black king while heading toward f6.', 'King support and king exclusion cooperate.', 'It checks the black king.', 'Kings do not give legal adjacent checks; the move restricts squares without checking.'),
    ),
    ('A checking intermediate move can reverse the intended pawn order.', 'Verify that the king can recapture if the rook sacrifices for the leading pawn.'),
    ('Lead with the correct pawn.', 'Support and restrict with the king.', 'Calculate rook checks before committing the next push.'))

teach('rook-v-minor', (285, 287),
    ('Defend rook against knight by keeping the pieces coordinated.', 'Exploit separation with a concrete double attack.'),
    (
        ('The normal defence is coordination', '''Without pawns, a rook does not normally force a win against a well-placed knight or bishop. The weaker king and minor piece must cooperate so that rook checks do not separate them. A knight close to its king can cover escape squares and avoid a free capture; an isolated knight near the edge may have too few safe jumps. The chapter’s diagram 411 starts White Ke6 and Rc7 against Black Kf8 and Ng8. Black’s two pieces are near each other and the source treats this arrangement as drawn.'''),
        ('Watch the defensive choice, not only White’s first move', '''Ra7, ...Nh6 and Rh7 ask the knight where it will go. Returning ...Ng8 preserves the coordination. The replay instead examines the mistake ...Ng4, which moves the knight away from its king. Now Rh3 restricts the knight’s exits; it does not attack g4 directly. The rook covers e3, h2 and h6, while the white king controls e5 and f6. The remaining knight retreat ...Nf2 allows a checking fork. King moves also fail: ...Ke8 allows Rh8 mate, while ...Kg7 or ...Kg8 allows Rg3, pinning the knight to its king. The quiet rook move prepares different answers to different defences.'''),
        ('Calculate the punishment through the last capture', '''The shown ...Nf2 chooses the remaining apparently safe knight retreat, but Rf3+ checks the king and attacks f2 along the file. Black must answer the check and then Rxf2 wins the knight. If Black chooses a different branch after Rh3, the source supplies other concrete methods, including ...Ke8 allowing Rh8 mate and ...Kg7 allowing Rg3. Name the threatened follow-up against each defensive move and verify its line. “The knight is separated” is a warning sign; the forcing sequence is the proof that this particular separation loses.'''),
        ('Bishop endings need a colour check as well', '''A bishop can often defend from a safe corner arrangement, but a badly placed king and bishop can be trapped. Diagrams 412–414 compare bishop colour, rook pawns, king escape squares and pawn sacrifices that open checking lines. The rook side should restrict before chasing; the minor-piece side should avoid being driven into the wrong corner without coordination. In your games, keep checks that improve the rook’s position and reject checks that merely let the pieces reunite. Once the minor piece is captured, use the basic king-and-rook mating method rather than continuing to search for the old tactic.'''),
        ('Other pawnless material needs its own result test', '''The chapter’s survey also compares larger pawnless armies. Rook and bishop against rook, or queen and knight against queen, may offer practical chances but do not automatically force a win against a safe king. Queen against two minor pieces depends strongly on their coordination; two mutually supporting knights can deny the attacking king entry, while bishop and knight can be harder to keep together. Queen against rook and pawn may face a defensive barrier when the rook excludes the attacking king from the pawn. Two knights against an enemy pawn have special winning cases because the pawn supplies waiting moves and avoids stalemate. These are advanced material families, not a complete fortress or two-knight mating course; the practical lesson is to check the exact destination before removing the last pawns.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Which defence preserves the source’s normal king-and-knight arrangement after Rh7?', '...Ng8, returning the knight near its king.', 'The nearby pieces continue to protect each other’s routes.', '...Ng4, because the knight is more central.', 'Centralization alone does not compensate for losing king support and tactical safety.'),
        ('Why does Rf3+ win after ...Nf2?', 'It checks the king and attacks the knight on the same file.', 'Black cannot answer the check and move the knight on the same turn.', 'It is checkmate immediately.', 'Black has legal king replies; the material win comes on the following capture.'),
    ),
    ('A separated knight needs an actual tactical refutation before a forced-win claim.', 'Bishop corner defence depends on square colour and king access.'),
    ('Keep the minor piece near its king.', 'Look for a checking double attack.', 'Continue to the actual capture or mate.'))

teach('wrong-bishop', (272, 273),
    ('Check whether a bishop can control a rook pawn’s promotion corner.', 'Verify stalemate after a tempting pawn advance.'),
    (
        ('The last pawn changes the material question', '''King, bishop and pawn can have plenty of material but still fail to promote a rook pawn if the defending king occupies the corner and the bishop controls the wrong colour. For an h-pawn promoting on h8, the corner is dark. A light-squared bishop cannot attack it, no matter how many moves it gets. This lesson is an original simplifying extension of the chapter’s defensive exchange and blockade ideas, not a claim that the chapter reproduces this exact diagram. Its purpose is to make the promotion-square test memorable.'''),
        ('Inventory every controlled square', '''White has Kg6, Bh5 and h6; Black has Kh8. The bishop is light-squared and the promotion corner h8 is dark. White’s king covers g7 and h7 but cannot approach h8 because kings may not become adjacent. The pawn on h6 still leaves Black a possible king move on g8. Before advancing it, say what the pawn on h7 will attack: g8, not h8. This attack removes the king’s last escape without putting the king in check.'''),
        ('The immediate result of h7', '''After h7, Black has the turn. Its king cannot move to g8 because of the pawn, g7 because of White’s king, or h7 because the pawn is protected by Kg6. There is no other black piece or pawn to move. Kh8 is not checked by either the king, bishop or pawn. The game therefore ends in stalemate immediately. White cannot say “I will promote next move,” because there is no next move once an ending condition has occurred. The exercise deliberately asks for the stalemate move so you practise identifying that condition.'''),
        ('Use the exception when deciding exchanges', '''When defending, trade other pawns if that leaves only the wrong-coloured rook pawn and you can reach its corner. When attacking, preserve another pawn, deny the corner earlier, or check whether your king can catch the defender before it arrives. This exception requires the actual shelter: a distant defending king may not be able to reach the corner in time. It also differs from rook against bishop with a pawn, where the rook can check on both colours. Always assess the pieces that remain after an exchange rather than treating “bishop and extra pawn” as one universal result.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Can Bh5 ever attack h8 by manoeuvring?', 'No; the bishop stays on light squares and h8 is dark.', 'Bishop colour is permanent.', 'Yes, if it goes through the centre first.', 'Travelling through the centre does not change a bishop’s square colour.'),
        ('Why is h7 a draw rather than an immediate win?', 'It stalemates Black before another white turn occurs.', 'There is no legal black move and Kh8 is not checked.', 'A rook pawn cannot become a queen.', 'A rook pawn promotes normally if the game continues and its final square is legally available.'),
    ),
    ('Check the corner colour rather than the bishop’s apparent distance.', 'The defender must actually reach the shelter; the exception is not universal.'),
    ('Locate the corner.', 'Compare bishop colour.', 'Count the defender’s legal moves after the push.'))

teach('queen-endings', (287, 288),
    ('Reduce defensive checking and pinning squares with a central queen.', 'Test how the defending king interferes with its own queen.'),
    (
        ('The promotion threat is not the whole battle', '''Queen endings are full of checks, skewers and pins. An advanced pawn may be unable to move because moving its king exposes check, or because the defending queen can pin it to the king. A strong attacking queen often needs a central post that controls the defender’s checking squares while maintaining threats. Diagram 415 has White Kg7, Qd3 and f7 against Black Kb4 and Qe7. White wants Kg8 and f8=Q, but must first remove the defence ...Qe6, a diagonal pin against the king and pawn.'''),
        ('A quiet queen move can do more than another check', '''Qf5 occupies a central diagonal and controls e6. It also changes the network of possible checks and queen exchanges. After the source’s ...Qa7, Qb1+ drives the king from b4 and controls the first rank, helping prevent ...Qg1+. Kg8 can then support promotion without allowing the original e6 pin or a useful pin from a2. Before playing the quiet move, calculate Black’s checking candidates; a centralizing queen that overlooks a perpetual check has not improved the practical result.'''),
        ('Examine alternatives by their geometry', '''The source compares ...Qb7, met by Qb1+, and ...Qc7, met by Qg4+ before Kg8. The point is to deny the queen’s checking or pinning destination with tempo. Black’s king on b4 gets in the way of some queen routes and can be checked at the wrong moment. Move that king to a4 and Black’s resources increase noticeably: ...Qb7 may now open useful manoeuvring possibilities. A king far from the passed pawn can still be decisive because queens operate across the entire board.'''),
        ('Practical defence and the limit of the example', '''The defender should seek close or rear checks, a diagonal pin of the pawn, or a king square that avoids tactical queen exchanges. The attacker should centralize, shelter the king and advance only when the checks are controlled. Centre pawns often give better winning chances than knight pawns because the defending king has fewer remote safe squares, but exact piece placement decides. Our replay stops once the key coordination is established, not at a fabricated promotion against cooperative checks. In your games continue calculating until you know what happens after the opponent’s most forcing check.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('What does Qf5 prevent before Kg8?', 'The defensive queen pin from e6.', 'Qf5 controls e6 along the diagonal.', 'Every possible black queen check anywhere.', 'The move reduces important resources, but every remaining check still needs calculation.'),
        ('Why can moving Black’s king from b4 to a4 help the defence?', 'It avoids some checking tempos and frees queen manoeuvring routes.', 'A remote king can obstruct its own queen in a queen ending.', 'Kings become stronger on every a-file square.', 'There is no general edge bonus; the particular checking geometry changes.'),
    ),
    ('A seventh-rank pawn may still be held by checks and pins.', 'Re-evaluate if the defending king moves off the queen’s routes.'),
    ('Centralize with a purpose.', 'Remove the pinning square.', 'Calculate the most forcing defensive check.'))

teach('rook-tactics', (288, 289),
    ('Recognize a mate threat combined with a rook attack.', 'Avoid declaring equal-rook endings drawn before inspecting tactics.'),
    (
        ('A theoretical draw still contains losing positions', '''King and rook against king and rook is overwhelmingly a drawn material family. That statement assumes reasonable piece placement and competent defence. A king trapped on an edge and a loose rook can create an immediate exception. Do a tactical scan before relying on the material label: checks, captures, mate threats and unprotected pieces. The scan is especially important when a king move opens a rook’s line or attacks a rook while improving the mating net. Endgame kings can attack pieces just as minor pieces can.'''),
        ('Two threats made by one quiet king move', '''Diagram 417 has White Kf7 and Rf3 against Black Kh8 and Rh5. Kg6 attacks the rook on h5 and threatens Rf8 mate. After that rook check, White’s king would cover h7 and g7 while the rook controls the eighth rank, so the king could not escape. Black must address the mating threat or give up the rook. In the displayed ...Kg8 branch, Black saves its king’s escape but leaves Rh5 available for Kxh5. White wins a rook because Black cannot solve both jobs at once.'''),
        ('Verify the opponent’s non-king resources', '''A mate threat is not checkmate and the defender is not obliged to choose the shown king move. Check whether the rook can capture the threatening rook, interpose on the future checking line, check the attacking king, or return to a protected square while stopping mate. In this sparse diagram the forcing geometry defeats those attempts, but a single extra pawn or a different rook square could supply a defence. The exercise specifies Kg6’s two exact threats; it is a calculation checkpoint, not a statement that all rook endings reward a central king move.'''),
        ('Transfer the habit to practical rook endings', '''The nearby source diagram 416 uses a rook move and an edge mating threat to win material. Practical games in the later chapter show checks that force a king away from a passed pawn or pawn pushes that threaten mate. Once the tactic has won the rook, stop applying the old drawing label and switch to king-and-rook versus king technique. From the defending side, keep the rook protected or active enough to check from a safe distance, and avoid corner confinement when an escape toward the centre is available. Piece safety and king mobility remain connected until the final move.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('What are the two threats after Kg6?', 'Kxh5 and Rf8 mate.', 'The king attacks the rook while the rook threatens an eighth-rank mate.', 'Two simultaneous checks on Kh8.', 'Kg6 is quiet; it creates threats without checking the king.'),
        ('Why must you inspect tactics in a normally drawn material ending?', 'Bad placement can allow an immediate mate or material loss.', 'A theoretical material tendency does not make every legal position drawn.', 'Equal material forces a draw under the rules.', 'Chess has no rule declaring every equal-material position drawn.'),
    ),
    ('Do not confuse a mating threat with checkmate.', 'Always inspect forcing rook checks as defensive alternatives.'),
    ('Scan tactics before using the material label.', 'Name both threats.', 'Calculate until the rook is actually won.'))

teach('cutoff-passer', (289, 290),
    ('Displace a king before pushing a passed pawn.', 'Use the rook to control the promotion rank while the king is distant.'),
    (
        ('The defender’s next king square can be the whole issue', '''Rook and pawn against rook usually needs king support, but a cut-off defending king sometimes lets the rook and pawn make progress alone. In diagram 419 White has Kh5, Ra5 and c6 against Ke8 and Re6. White’s king is far from c8, so the rook must do the supporting work. The tempting c7 fails to ...Kd7: Black’s king approaches the pawn with tempo and takes over the blockade. Before every pawn advance, locate the opposing king’s best approach square.'''),
        ('Check first to change that approach', '''Ra8+ forces the king off the eighth rank. From e8 it cannot answer this check by occupying d7, because d7 is attacked by the c6 pawn. After the illustrative ...Kf7, c7 threatens promotion supported by the rook along the eighth rank. This is a move-order technique: the same pawn advance becomes effective after a forcing change in the enemy king’s location. Had the king started on d8, its escape and attack routes would differ. The relative direction of the king, not just its distance, is part of the pattern.'''),
        ('Separate promotion from keeping the promoted piece', '''Black’s ...Re5+ in the replay is a practical checking try. White escapes with Kg4, then after ...Rc5 promotes c8=Q. A rook behind the pawn cannot attack through the pawn before it promotes. If Black then captures the new queen with ...Rxc8, White’s rook on a8 recaptures along the rank. The conversion is therefore not “promotion ends the game”; it is a concrete sequence that either keeps the queen or removes the defender’s rook. Check every capture on the promotion square before making that claim.'''),
        ('A cut-off king can also lose without the check', '''Diagram 418 illustrates a rook on the seventh rank barring the king while a b-pawn advances. Its lesson complements the check-first idea: sometimes a permanent rank barrier replaces nearby king support. Most rook-and-pawn endings still require the king, and a well-placed defensive rook can create counterchecks or attack the pawn before the barrier is ready. Use these positions as exceptions with identifiable conditions. In your games ask which line excludes the king, whether the pawn is safe on its next square, and how your rook will support the final promotion.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why not play c7 immediately in diagram 419?', 'Black can approach with ...Kd7 before its king has been displaced.', 'The next king square is available only before the forcing check changes the position.', 'A pawn on c6 may never move to c7.', 'The move is legal; its timing permits a defensive response.'),
        ('Why can ...Rxc8 after promotion fail?', 'Ra8 can recapture on c8 along the clear eighth rank.', 'The rook supports the promotion square even though White’s king is far away.', 'New queens cannot be captured on their first turn.', 'Promoted pieces can be captured normally; the recapture makes the difference.'),
    ),
    ('Include the defender’s king approach in the pawn calculation.', 'Promotion must survive capture or gain enough in the exchange.'),
    ('Find the king’s blockade square.', 'Use a forcing check to deny it.', 'Trace the support on the promotion rank.'))

teach('bridge', (270, 292),
    ('Prepare a fourth-rank rook shield before leaving the pawn’s shelter.', 'Calculate the bridge through the defender’s possible rook capture.'),
    (
        ('The winning position still contains an obstacle', '''In the Lucena-type diagram 426 White has Kg8, Re1 and g7 against Kd7 and Rh2. The pawn is one step from promotion, but White’s own king occupies g8. Moving the king out gives Black rear checks, so simply announcing that the pawn queens next is insufficient. Black’s king is excluded from the pawn area; White can use that time to prepare a rook shield. The bridge is a sequence that gives the king a route out and then interposes the rook against the final checking file.'''),
        ('Prepare before the first check', '''Re4 puts the rook on the fourth rank, where it can later slide across to g4. After ...Rh1, Kf7 frees the promotion square. Black checks with ...Rf1+, and the king goes Kg6, ...Rg1+, Kf6, ...Rf1+ and Kg5. At each move ask where the next check comes from and whether the king remains close enough to support g7. The king is not wandering: it is leading the rook onto the file where a fourth-rank interposition can protect it.'''),
        ('The bridge is complete only when the check is blocked safely', '''After ...Rg1+, Rg4 places the rook between the black rook and the king on g5. Black no longer has a free g-file check. If ...Rxg4+ follows, Kxg4 removes Black’s rook; the king on d7 is too far from g7’s promotion square. If Black declines, White has time to promote or improve the shield. This last capture check is essential: a bridge rook hanging without king support would not solve the ending. Verify the line g1–g4–g5 and the legal king recapture before declaring success.'''),
        ('Compare the conditions before copying the bridge', '''The source discusses a defending king on d6 instead of d7: its apparent attack on Re4 permits a different rook placement and releases the white king’s route. Other positions may have a rook pawn, a closer defending king or insufficient cutting-off distance, requiring another method. The Botvinnik–Boleslavsky practical ending reaches the same bridge idea after a long conversion involving an outside passer. Study the bridge as a destination you can aim for, but compare both kings, the pawn file and the rooks before assuming you have reached it.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why is Re4 played before Kf7?', 'The rook must be ready to shield the king after the rear checks begin.', 'The preparation supplies Rg4 at the end of the king route.', 'A rook can only block checks from rank four.', 'Other ranks can block checks, but this arrangement specifically needs the fourth-rank bridge.'),
        ('What happens if Black captures Rg4 with check?', 'Kxg4 removes the rook and the distant king cannot stop promotion.', 'The bridge rook is protected by the king on g5.', 'White loses because interposing pieces may not be captured.', 'Interposing pieces can be captured; the legal recapture is the point.'),
    ),
    ('A seventh-rank pawn cannot promote through its own king.', 'A shield must survive the opponent’s capture or win the resulting ending.'),
    ('Prepare the rook shield.', 'Lead the checks toward it.', 'Verify the final interposition and recapture.'))

teach('rook-pawn-shelter', (292, 294),
    ('Preserve a shelter square beside a rook pawn.', 'Distinguish a king’s shelter from a pawn’s apparent progress.'),
    (
        ('The edge removes an escape file', '''A rook pawn leaves its king only one side on which to manoeuvre. This makes rook-and-rook-pawn endings more sensitive to exact shelter squares than many central-pawn endings. In diagram 427 White has Kc5, Ra8 and a6 against Kf7 and Ra1. The attacking rook is in front of its own pawn, so the king must help free the rook and escape rear checks. Pushing a7 immediately occupies the square the king needs and gives the defender a familiar drawing arrangement.'''),
        ('Use a7 with the king before using it with the pawn', '''Kb6 meets ...Rb1+ with Ka7. Because the pawn remains on a6, the king has a7 as shelter from the checking rook. The rook can then leave a8 with Rb8 while the king stays near the pawn. After ...Rc1, Kb7 is the precise move in the source’s line; an immediate Rb6 would let ...Kd7 establish a hold. Replay slowly enough to distinguish a safe king step from a rook move that appears to defend the pawn but gives the opponent the wrong tempo.'''),
        ('Shelter is temporary, so preserve the move order', '''After ...Rb1+ and Ka8, Black returns to c1 and White finally advances a7. The king has used the shelter, the rook has cleared a8, and the pieces now have another route against the checks. The source continues with further careful king manoeuvres and rook checks; our replay stops at the essential transition. Do not infer that any rook move now wins instantly. The defender still has checking attempts, and the precise relation between the distant king and White’s rook determines whether a proposed shield works.'''),
        ('The defensive companion position', '''Diagram 428 moves White’s king far away to g2. Black can set up a sixth-rank rook barrier by checking from the fifth rank and then moving to the sixth. A single extra king tempo can defeat that system, so the attacker’s king distance is part of the theory. In your games resist the urge to push because the pawn is passed: first name where your king will hide, how the rook will leave the promotion square and what checking method the defender can establish. From the defending seat, prioritize an active barrier over passive waiting directly in front of the pawn.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why is a7 held back at the start?', 'The king needs a7 as shelter after ...Rb1+.', 'The pawn must leave the square free until the king has used it.', 'A rook pawn may only advance when its rook is behind it.', 'That is not a rule; the shelter geometry determines the move order.'),
        ('Does Rb8 alone finish the entire source ending?', 'No; further checks and precise king steps remain.', 'The move solves one job, freeing a8, while the defender keeps practical resources.', 'Yes; clearing a promotion square always wins immediately.', 'The pawn still has to advance and the exposed king still has to answer checks.'),
    ),
    ('Do not occupy your only shelter square too early.', 'A rook move that guards the pawn can still concede a critical king tempo.'),
    ('Identify the shelter.', 'Use it with the king.', 'Clear the promotion square before the final pawn push.'))

teach('sixth-rank', (293, 295),
    ('Build the Philidor sixth-rank barrier.', 'Switch to rear checks as soon as the pawn removes the king’s shelter.'),
    (
        ('Defend the king’s destination, not just the pawn', '''Diagram 429 gives White Kf5, Rh7 and e5 against Black Ke8 and Ra6. Black’s king is in front of the pawn, covering e8. The rook on the sixth rank keeps the attacking king off e6 and f6, where it could support a decisive advance or mating threat. This is the essential Philidor defensive arrangement. The king and rook divide the work: the king protects the promotion area while the rook prevents the stronger king from gaining its ideal shelter.'''),
        ('Wait on the barrier while the attacker manoeuvres', '''After Rb7, ...Rc6 keeps the rook on the sixth rank. Black need not chase the white rook or give a check that abandons the barrier. Ask what White’s king can actually achieve if it cannot cross that rank. A rook may shuffle along an open rank while preserving the same restriction; do not confuse such a waiting move with inactivity. The defender is actively denying the square that would make White’s pawn dangerous, while keeping its king in the only place that can hold the promotion blockade.'''),
        ('The pawn advance is the switching signal', '''When White pushes e6, that pawn occupies the potential shelter square in front of the king. Black should immediately move behind the pawn with ...Rc1 and prepare distant checks. Now a king approaching e6 cannot stand on its own pawn, and a king moving away can be checked on a neighbouring file. For instance, after Kg5, ...Rg1+ supplies the rear check. Before choosing the rook’s retreat, check that it cannot be captured and that it retains enough distance to check along the required files.'''),
        ('How the attacker can punish a premature release', '''The source shows a rook abandoning the sixth-rank barrier before e6, allowing Kf6 and a winning pawn advance. The error is giving the king its shelter while the pawn is still behind it. Conversely, clinging to the sixth rank after the pawn advances can leave the rook badly placed for checks. Learn both phases as one method, with a precise changeover condition. In your own games, identify the attacking king’s desired square, keep the barrier until the pawn occupies it, then look for rear checks. Compare the exact position if the pawn is a rook pawn or the defending king has been cut off.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why stay on the sixth rank before e6?', 'To deny the attacking king a useful entry and shelter square.', 'The rook’s barrier prevents the king from supporting the pawn’s next phase.', 'A rook on rank six automatically attacks the promotion square.', 'Promotion control belongs mainly to Ke8 here, while the rook restricts the king.'),
        ('What signals the switch to rear checks?', 'The pawn advances to e6 and occupies its own king’s potential shelter.', 'That geometry makes distant checks effective.', 'White’s rook moves to any new file.', 'A rook shuffle does not by itself change the barrier’s purpose.'),
    ),
    ('Do not abandon the barrier before the king’s shelter is occupied.', 'Do not keep the barrier mechanically after the pawn has advanced.'),
    ('King in front.', 'Rook across the sixth.', 'Pawn advances: switch behind and check.'))

teach('lateral-checks', (295, 296),
    ('Choose the long side for the defending rook and the short side for its king.', 'Count intervening files rather than relying on a vague sense of distance.'),
    (
        ('The pawn divides the board unequally', '''An e-pawn leaves a-, b-, c- and d-files on one side and f-, g- and h-files on the other. The larger region is the long side, the smaller the short side. In diagram 430 White has Ke8, Rf4 and e7 against Kg7 and Ra7, Black to move. The defending king stands on the short side and its rook on the long side. This keeps the king from blocking the rook’s rank checks and gives those checks enough room to operate.'''),
        ('Checking distance is a practical resource', '''Ra8+ and the following rank checks keep the attacking king moving. From the a-file, three files lie between rook and e-pawn: b, c and d. Even when White approaches c6, the rook can check from a6 without being captured immediately. The king cannot both run all the way to the distant rook and remain near e7. If it abandons the pawn’s defence, the rook may capture the pawn instead. Count the intervening files explicitly; “three squares away” can mean different things if you accidentally count the occupied endpoints.'''),
        ('One file nearer gives the attacker a tempo', '''The contrasting replay uses diagram 430a, changing only Ra7 to Rb7. Now only c and d lie between rook and pawn. After the checking sequence Kc7 attacks a rook left on b7, forcing it to retreat. Ra4 then prepares a shield and supports the winning coordination; ...Rxa4 would allow e8=Q. Do not merely memorize that a-file is good and b-file bad. With an f-pawn the long side is larger, so the b-file may supply enough distance. The useful quantity is room relative to the pawn’s file.'''),
        ('Distance is necessary here, not sufficient everywhere', '''The defending king’s exact square and the attacking rook’s ability to interpose can overturn the general pattern. The source’s next diagrams change Kg7 to Kg8 or move the white rook, altering access to f6 or the pawn’s protection. These are the subject of the following lesson. In practice, first generate the standard setup, then calculate the attacker’s nearest approach, interposition and promotion threat. When attacking, force the checking rook closer or move your own rook to a useful shield rather than walking through the same checks without changing anything.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('How many files lie between an a-file rook and an e-pawn?', 'Three: b, c and d.', 'The occupied a- and e-files are endpoints, not intervening files.', 'Two: c and d.', 'That is the distance from b to e, the nearer and less effective source setup.'),
        ('Why place the defending king on the short side?', 'To leave the long side clear for distant rook checks.', 'The king should not obstruct its own rook’s checking routes.', 'The short side is always safest against every pawn.', 'Exact king access can still change the result, so the rule needs calculation.'),
    ),
    ('Count the files between rook and pawn correctly.', 'Recheck king access and attacking-rook shielding squares.'),
    ('Rook on the long side.', 'King on the short side.', 'Test the king’s approach before trusting the distance.'))

teach('lateral-exceptions', (296, 298),
    ('Compare king and attacking-rook placement before applying side-check theory.', 'Recognize a deliberate rook exchange into stalemate.'),
    (
        ('A familiar shell may hide a different result', '''The side-check method depends on more than a seventh-rank pawn and a distant rook. In the central-pawn source diagram 430b, moving the defending king from g7 to g8 gives White access to f6. White can walk through checks toward g6 and bring its rook into a shield. Diagram 430c instead alters the attacking rook to c6; that rook cannot keep the same protection and checking tempo, and Black can draw by a precise king approach. Compare every piece before importing the result of a remembered diagram.'''),
        ('A bishop pawn leaves another corner geometry', '''Our main replay is diagram 432a: White Kf8, Rg4 and f7 against Black Kh8 and Ra5, with Black to move. The pawn is on f rather than e, and White’s rook has no immediate ideal shield. Black checks from the a-file while White’s king tries e7, e6, f5, g6, h5 and h6. Keep asking whether White can interpose without losing the pawn, and whether Black’s king has a legal square if the rooks disappear. The second question is easy to overlook while counting checking distance.'''),
        ('The defender can welcome the exchange', '''After ...Ra6+, White finally plays Rg6 to block the checks. Black answers ...Rxg6+, and Kxg6 leaves only White Kg6/f7 against Black Kh8. Kh8 is not checked. The pawn attacks g8; White’s king controls g7 and h7. Black has no legal move, so the position is stalemate. The capture sequence is therefore an intentional defensive route into a draw, not a blunder caused by failing to count rooks. Always inspect the ending after the last recapture, not merely the material balance during the exchange.'''),
        ('Use exceptions as a comparison habit', '''From the attacker’s seat, ask whether your rook can shield on a different file while retaining the pawn, or whether a king route avoids the corner stalemate. From the defender’s seat, do not trade automatically: the exact ending must be checked with the side to move. The source also warns that a rook check placed too close on h6 can lose the checking route. A practical comparison list is pawn file and rank, both kings, both rooks, available interpositions and the legal moves after a possible exchange. That list explains exceptions instead of treating them as disconnected memorization.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why is ...Rxg6+ not a losing rook trade in the replay?', 'The forced material reduction leaves Black stalemated after Kxg6.', 'The final legal-move test matters more than White’s extra pawn.', 'A king may capture a rook only when the game is drawn.', 'King captures follow ordinary legality; the resulting stalemate supplies the draw.'),
        ('What must be compared before reusing a side-check result?', 'Every piece’s square, the pawn’s file/rank and the available shields.', 'Small changes can open a king route or remove pawn protection.', 'Only the number of rooks.', 'Equal rook counts do not preserve checking geometry.'),
    ),
    ('A blocking rook move may permit a drawing exchange.', 'A pawn-file change can alter both king escape and stalemate geometry.'),
    ('Compare the exact pieces.', 'Inspect the proposed shield.', 'Count legal moves after the last recapture.'))

teach('seventh-bishop-pawn', (297, 298),
    ('Recognize useful attacking-rook support and shielding zones.', 'Regroup with a forcing check before supporting promotion.'),
    (
        ('The attacking rook’s square decides what the king can do', '''With an f-pawn on the seventh, the defender has a large long side for checking, but that does not guarantee a draw. An attacking rook on the eighth rank can support promotion across the rank; a rook on a useful central file can interpose while retaining the pawn. Diagram 431 has White Kf8, Rd8 and f7 against Black Kh7 and Ra7, Black to move. White threatens Ke8, freeing f8 for promotion while the rook controls the last rank.'''),
        ('Use a check to move the rook into its next job', '''The source’s ...Kg6 tries to approach the pawn before White completes that threat. Rd6+ makes Black answer a check while White improves its rook’s relation to the pawn. After ...Kh7, Rf6 goes behind f7 and provides direct support. The sequence joins the active eighth-rank position to the familiar rear-rook arrangement. Before Rd6+, pause to ask whether the rook can be captured or the check interposed; then verify that the resulting Rf6 still lets the king escape the checks instead of trapping it in front of its own pawn.'''),
        ('“Winning zone” means usable geometry', '''The source lists many winning rook squares and explicitly excludes some near misses such as d6 in the wrong initial arrangement. A rook that seems close may fail because the defender can approach with ...Kg7 or capture the pawn with tempo. Do not teach yourself the zone as a coloured patch without understanding the jobs. Ask whether the rook protects the promotion rank, directly supports the pawn, or can block a side check safely. The answer explains why moving the rook one file can change the theoretical result.'''),
        ('The defender still needs an exact king move', '''Diagram 431a puts White’s rook on e7 and Black’s rook on a1, with the defending king on g6. The resource ...Kf6! can hold: checking from behind and the side denies the white king’s escape and threatens the pawn. This is a useful warning against announcing a win merely because your king and pawn have reached the seventh and eighth ranks. In a real game, identify your rook’s next two jobs, calculate the defender’s nearest king approach, and prefer a forcing regrouping when it removes a defensive option. Reaching a known support arrangement is more reliable than shuffling with no destination.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('What is the job of Rd6+ after ...Kg6?', 'Gain a forcing tempo while preparing Rf6 behind the pawn.', 'The check connects active rook placement with direct pawn support.', 'Win Black’s rook immediately.', 'Black’s rook is on a7, not on a capturable square along Rd6’s line.'),
        ('What does a useful attacking-rook zone describe?', 'Squares that support promotion or allow a safe shield against checks.', 'The geometry, not the colour of a memorized region, supplies the method.', 'Any square closer to the pawn than the defending rook.', 'Closeness alone can leave the rook unable to shield or preserve the pawn.'),
    ),
    ('Check the defender’s king approach before claiming a win.', 'A nearby rook square may still be a theoretical exception.'),
    ('Identify the rook’s useful zone.', 'Regroup with tempo.', 'Keep promotion support and a king escape route together.'))

teach('sixth-bishop-pawn', (298, 300),
    ('Use side checks and back-rank pressure against a sixth-rank bishop pawn.', 'Recognize when a natural defensive rook move loses a critical tempo.'),
    (
        ('One rank back gives the defender more options', '''A pawn on f6 is dangerous, but unlike a pawn on f7 it still needs another move before the promotion threat becomes immediate. That spare turn can let the defending king approach or let the rook switch between side checks and rear pressure. Diagram 433 has White Kf7, Re3 and f6 against Black Kh7 and Ra5, Black to move. The source identifies it as drawn with accurate defence. Do not infer the result from the pawn’s rank alone: the exposed white king and the rook’s shielding square are crucial.'''),
        ('Follow the first defensive switch exactly', '''...Ra7+ makes White deal with the king’s exposure. After Re7, ...Ra8 keeps useful back-rank pressure and checking access. Staying on a6 at this moment would be an error: Kf8+ and the supported pawn route can then win because Black lacks a saving g-file check. The lesson is not “all a-file squares are equivalent.” A rook needs both the correct distance and the correct rank for its next checking move. Before advancing the replay, predict the response to White’s Kf8 and identify the rook move that preserves it.'''),
        ('The attacker’s new shield requires a new answer', '''Re6 changes the defensive problem. The source’s ...Rb8 is one drawing move, with ...Kh6 another. Ordinary a-file rook moves now allow Kf8, and ...Rc8 permits a forcing rook check and king escape in the source’s analysis. The board exercise asks specifically for ...Rb8 so that you practise this change of job. It does not pretend there is only one legal drawing move. Accurate defensive play is often a sequence of reassessments: a good square two moves ago may become the wrong square after the attacker changes its shield.'''),
        ('Compare fifth- and sixth-rank cases', '''Diagram 433a demonstrates a draw even with only two intervening files when the pawn is on f6, provided ...Kg6 and the later ...Rc8 are timed correctly. Diagram 434 starts the pawn on f5 and requires ...Ra6 before the same family of positions is reached. These exceptions explain why a slogan about three files cannot replace calculation. In practice classify the pawn’s rank, find the attacker’s next shelter, then compare checks, pawn attacks and king approaches. As the stronger side, alter the shield or advance with a forcing threat; as defender, respond to the new geometry instead of repeating the old rook shuffle.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why is ...Ra6 wrong after ...Ra7+ Re7?', 'It allows the king’s Kf8+ route without the needed saving check.', 'The source’s exact rank choice preserves the next checking resource.', 'Rooks may not move to rank six when a pawn is on f6.', 'The move is legal; it fails tactically and positionally in this particular arrangement.'),
        ('Is ...Rb8 the only legal drawing move after Re6?', 'No; ...Kh6 also draws in the source.', 'The exercise narrows its task to the rook switch rather than claiming uniqueness.', 'Yes; every other legal move must lose.', 'The source supplies another drawing king move, so a uniqueness claim would be false.'),
    ),
    ('Reassess after the attacking rook changes its shield.', 'Checking-distance rules have pawn-rank and king-square exceptions.'),
    ('Classify the pawn rank.', 'Keep the next checking resource.', 'Change the rook’s job when the shield changes.'))

teach('frontal-checks', (300, 302),
    ('Use checking distance in front of a fourth-rank pawn.', 'Keep the king covering the attacker’s invasion squares.'),
    (
        ('Rear checks need space too', '''When a pawn is only on the fourth rank, a rook behind it has little distance for useful rear checks against the escorting king. Frontal checks become more important: the rook stands ahead of the pawn and alternates files to keep the king exposed. Diagram 436 gives White Kg3, Rf1 and g4 against Black Ke5 and Rg8. Black’s rook has room on the eighth rank, and its centralized king covers important entry squares. The defence depends on both pieces, not on the rook checking alone forever.'''),
        ('Follow the checks before pushing the pawn', '''White tries Kh4, ...Rh8+, Kg5, ...Rg8+, Kh5, ...Rh8+ and Kg6. Each check comes from in front and forces another king move. White has not gained a square where its own rook or pawn shelters the king. After ...Rg8+, the king must retreat rather than advance g5 immediately. Pause before every check and test whether the king can approach or capture the rook. The file-switching works because the rook is distant enough and the black king limits the escape route.'''),
        ('The king’s active square is part of the barrier', '''A different move order begins with a rook check or an attempted rook shield. The source shows Black answering with king centralization and timely rook checks, then returning to the g-file to restrain g5. A careless king retreat can let the pawn advance with support and change the entire result. In the neighbouring diagram 435 the white rook already protects the pawn from f4 and the black king is farther away on e7; with White to move, a king approach wins. Compare those pieces before saying the two positions “look the same.”'''),
        ('Attack the system, not an individual check', '''From White’s seat, look for a safe rook shield, a way to cut the defending king farther off, or a pawn advance that creates shelter without losing the pawn. From Black’s seat, preserve frontal distance and cover the rook’s potential invasion squares with the king. A rook exchange must be evaluated as a pawn ending; the source’s companion examples show the move order deciding whether such an exchange wins. In a practical game, explain both halves of the defence aloud: which rook check will keep the king exposed, and which king square prevents the attacker’s rook from creating a shelter?'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why are frontal checks useful against a fourth-rank pawn?', 'There is substantial room in front, while rear checking space is limited.', 'The pawn’s rank changes where the rook has effective distance.', 'Rooks behind pawns are illegal before rank five.', 'Rear placement is legal; it may simply be less useful in this geometry.'),
        ('What besides the checking rook makes the draw work?', 'The defending king covers key invasion and shelter squares.', 'The king prevents White from regrouping freely around the checks.', 'The number of moves already played.', 'The move count is not the source of this particular defensive method.'),
    ),
    ('Do not compare frontal-check positions without comparing both kings.', 'A premature king retreat can release the pawn advance.'),
    ('Use the available frontal distance.', 'Keep the king active.', 'Check the attacker’s possible rook shields.'))

teach('frontal-cutoff', (301, 302),
    ('Compare the room available beside a bishop pawn and a knight pawn.', 'Time the rook transfer behind the pawn after the king escapes checks.'),
    (
        ('A cut-off king is a timed disadvantage', '''A rook on an open file can stop the defending king from approaching a pawn. The effect depends on how far away the king is and how quickly it can return if the rook changes files. Diagram 437 has White Kf3, Rd1 and f4 against Black Kc4 and Rf8. Black’s king is cut off by the d-file and cannot reach the pawn quickly. White still must escape frontal checks and place its rook behind f4; merely counting the cutting-off files does not finish the calculation.'''),
        ('Use the spare outer file', '''Kg4, ...Rg8+, Kf5, ...Rf8+, Kg5 and ...Rg8+ bring White’s king to the key Kh6 move. This h6 escape exists because the pawn is on f4, leaving two files to its right. In the analogous g-pawn arrangement the edge removes the corresponding outer route. Kh6 is therefore not a random advance toward the enemy rook: it walks around the frontal-checking system. Before making it, inspect both the rook’s line and the enemy king’s distance to ensure that the apparently exposed king is actually safe.'''),
        ('Change the rook’s job only when the king has made room', '''After ...Rf8, Rf1 goes behind the pawn. This gives up the d-file barrier, but Black’s king is too far away to exploit that release immediately. White’s king continues Kg7 and Kg6, attacking a rook on f5 and gaining the tempo for f5. The replay uses ...Kd5 as one legal king approach after Rf1; the source allows other king moves. The method is a timed transfer: use one rook job to win a king route, then replace it with pawn support before the defender can catch up.'''),
        ('The nearby knight-pawn hold', '''Diagram 436a places the pawn on g4 and cuts Black’s king off by two files, yet Black can draw by covering the white rook’s invasion squares and retaining frontal checks. The extra file of king room is missing. This comparison explains why “two files cut off wins” needs a pawn-file condition. In your games trace the actual king escape squares first, then count how many moves the released defender needs. From the defending side, use your king to deny rook shelters rather than running toward the pawn along a blocked file. Both the route and the timing matter.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why is Kh6 the key route in the f-pawn example?', 'The bishop pawn leaves an extra outside file for the king to escape frontal checks.', 'The king has room that the analogous knight-pawn case lacks.', 'Kings may jump over checking rooks on the h-file.', 'A king never jumps; Kh6 is an ordinary safe adjacent move.'),
        ('Why can White release the d-file with Rf1?', 'The opposing king is still too far away to exploit the release before the pawn gains support.', 'The earlier cut-off bought a useful amount of time.', 'Once a king has been cut off, it may never cross that file again.', 'A rook’s departure removes the barrier; the remaining distance and tempi must be calculated.'),
    ),
    ('The number of cut-off files is not a complete result test.', 'Releasing the barrier requires a concrete time calculation.'),
    ('Find the outside king route.', 'Use the cut-off to gain time.', 'Transfer the rook behind the pawn when the route is ready.'))

teach('two-pawns', (290, 292),
    ('Overcome an active rank barrier before advancing two passers.', 'Choose the final pawn order to shelter the king and threaten mate.'),
    (
        ('Two pawns are not two free moves', '''Rook and two pawns against rook often wins, but an active defensive rook can make progress surprisingly difficult. Diagrams 422–424 show drawing exceptions involving checks, pawn captures and stalemate. Diagram 425 supplies a winning contrast: White Kh5, Ra7, g5 and h6 against Black Kg8 and Rb5. Black’s rook controls the fifth rank and checks an approaching king, while premature pawn pushes can expose pins or let the rook capture with check. White must change the rook geometry before cashing in the pawn advantage.'''),
        ('Contest the rank in stages', '''The replay begins Ra1 and Rf1, positioning the rook to use f4. Kg4 meets ...Ra4+ with Rf4, an interposition that offers a rook exchange and gains control of the important rank. After Black declines, Rf5 challenges the fifth rank directly. Trace the rook’s route a7–a1–f1–f4–f5 and give a job to every stop. The first retreat is purposeful because the final fifth-rank square cannot be reached safely by a direct king march through the checking barrier.'''),
        ('Only then change the pawn formation', '''After ...Ra4+, Kh5 and ...Ra1, Rb5 secures the fifth rank and Black retreats to a8. White can now play g6, ...Kh8 and h7. The source emphasizes that h7 is the correct next pawn: it shelters the king’s route to h6, after which g7 will be mate. The king must reach h6 first: an immediate g7+ would allow ...Kxg7. Before g6, check the rook’s potential side check; before h7, check whether the king still has shelter. Pawn order is tied to the rook’s position, not a general rule that the inner or outer pawn must always move first.'''),
        ('Defend by keeping the attacker’s jobs incompatible', '''Black wants to check the king while threatening a pawn, or force a rook exchange into a hold. White wants one rook move to provide both a shield and a safe pawn advance. In the drawing companions a pawn capture with check prevents the attacking king from consolidating, and a rook sacrifice may lead to stalemate. The winning replay shows how to remove those resources methodically. In your games locate the defensive barrier first, then identify a rook route that challenges it without losing either pawn. Recalculate after each check instead of treating extra pawns as permission to ignore the opponent.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('What is White’s first strategic task?', 'Remove or contest the black rook’s fifth-rank barrier.', 'The king and pawns cannot advance safely while the active checks retain their force.', 'Promote either pawn immediately.', 'The pawns are not yet on the seventh rank and the checks must be solved first.'),
        ('Why is Rf4 useful after ...Ra4+?', 'It blocks the check and helps gain the rank needed for progress.', 'The interposition has a positional job as well as answering check.', 'It checks Black’s king on g8.', 'A rook on f4 does not attack g8; its value here is shielding and rank control.'),
    ),
    ('A pawn may be pinned after an apparently useful rook check.', 'An extra pair of pawns does not remove stalemate or checking resources.'),
    ('Identify the barrier.', 'Build a useful rook shield.', 'Choose the pawn order only after the checks are controlled.'))

teach('counterpasser', (302, 303),
    ('Tie an enemy rook to a distant promotion threat.', 'Maintain active checks while preserving the exact king defence.'),
    (
        ('Counterplay can be worth a pawn deficit', '''A defender facing two connected passed pawns may survive by creating one far-advanced passer on the opposite wing. The attacker’s rook must then watch the promotion square instead of joining the king freely. Diagram 438 gives White Kh3, Ra5, g3 and h4 against Black Kg6, Rc2 and a3, with Black to move. Black’s a-pawn is not merely material to preserve: reaching a2 creates the continuing threat ...a1=Q and limits the jobs White’s rook can undertake.'''),
        ('Put the pawn, not the rook, on a2', '''...a2 is the essential first move. ...Ra2 would occupy the very square needed by the pawn and allow the attacker a different winning arrangement. After h5+ and ...Kf6, White’s king approaches h4, but ...Rh2+ starts active checking. The rook then returns to b2 and checks from b5 when the king approaches g5. At each white king move ask whether the a-pawn still prevents the rook from moving away, and at each black check verify that the rook is safe and retains the ability to attack the advanced pawns.'''),
        ('A new pawn formation needs a new king square', '''The source line reaches Kh4, ...Rb2 and g4. Black must now play ...Kf7, preserving the defensive formation against the connected pawns. A casual ...Rc2 allows h6+ and Kh5, leading to the winning scheme from the previous lesson. The advance of one white pawn changed which king square holds, even though the distant a2 threat remained. Good defence is not repeating two rook squares mechanically; it is using the counter-pawn to buy enough time for exact checks and king placement.'''),
        ('Recognize which connected pawn is in front', '''The chapter compares g4/h5 with g5/h4. When the inner pawn is farther advanced, it can shelter the king from horizontal checks; with the outer pawn ahead, that shelter may be missing. Similar geometry appeared in two pawns against a rook. Before assessing a two-wing rook ending, list the passed pawn’s rank, which connected pawn leads, the defending king’s route and the active rook checks. The source position is a draw with accurate play, not a guarantee that every distant counter-pawn is enough. From the attacking side, seek a safe shelter or an exchange that removes the counter-pawn without allowing the checking system to reset.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why choose ...a2 rather than ...Ra2?', 'The pawn’s promotion threat ties White’s rook to the a-file while Black’s rook stays active.', 'Occupying a2 with the rook blocks that essential pawn advance.', 'A rook on a2 would be stalemate.', 'The issue is the lost counterplay geometry, not an immediate lack of legal moves.'),
        ('Why must Black reassess after g4?', 'The new connected-pawn formation requires the precise king defence ...Kf7.', 'The counter-pawn buys time but does not answer every new threat by itself.', 'The a2 pawn has disappeared.', 'The pawn remains; White’s changed shelter and advance possibilities are what matter.'),
    ),
    ('Do not block your own essential counter-pawn.', 'Recheck the king square after the attacking pawns change formation.'),
    ('Create a real promotion threat.', 'Keep the rook checking actively.', 'Answer each new pawn formation precisely.'))

teach('transitions', (303, 305),
    ('Evaluate the exact material and king positions after an exchange.', 'Choose a known pawn-ending destination before simplifying.'),
    (
        ('An exchange is a choice of the next game', '''A practical endgame may change several times: major pieces disappear, then minor pieces, then the last rook or knight. Each transition replaces one set of plans with another. Before offering an exchange, visualize the resulting pieces, pawn structure and side to move. “I am ahead, so trade” is incomplete. Trading an active rook into a lost pawn race is bad; trading into a known leading-king pawn win may be excellent. The target ending should be part of your reason for the exchange, not something you discover after recapturing.'''),
        ('Check recapture legality before calculating the result', '''Our original position gives White Ke6, Rd1 and e5 against Black Kc8 and Rd8. Rxd8+ offers the last-rook trade, and ...Kxd8 is legal because White’s king on e6 does not attack d8. This square check matters: if the recapture square were e7 next to Ke6, the black king could not capture there. Never remove both rooks automatically on an imagined board. First verify the capture, the recapture and the king’s safety at the end of each ply.'''),
        ('Follow the destination far enough to know it', '''After the accepted trade, White retains a king on the sixth rank ahead of its pawn. Kf7 supports the promotion square; against the shown ...Kd7, e6+ and e7+ gain forcing tempi, and e8=Q+ completes the conversion. The replay shows one legal defensive branch through the pawn ending rather than asserting that Black has only those king moves. The important practical skill is knowing why this type of destination is favourable: the king leads the pawn and can protect the square where it becomes a queen.'''),
        ('Choose which pieces remain, not only how many disappear', '''Smyslov–Euwe in the source deliberately exchanges major pieces because White’s knights have useful work against a cramped bishop. Later exchanges change that ending again. Bishop versus knight should be judged against the open or closed pawn structure; with opposite bishops a trade may strengthen the defender’s blockade. Rook exchanges may remove active checking resources or instead leave an easily held pawn ending. In your games compare at least two destinations when possible, then select the one whose method you can explain. Preserve the pieces and pawns that keep useful targets, and remove the opponent’s pieces that supply counterplay.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('What should be visualized before the last-rook exchange?', 'The remaining kings, pawns and correct side to move.', 'Those determine whether the resulting theoretical ending is favourable.', 'Only the number of points being traded.', 'Equal point exchanges can enter either a won, drawn or lost pawn ending.'),
        ('Why is ...Kxd8 legal in the replay?', 'Ke6 does not attack d8.', 'The recapture square is two ranks away from the white king.', 'Kings may ignore attacks when recapturing a rook.', 'A king capture must obey the same safety rule as every king move.'),
    ),
    ('Check recapture legality before removing both pieces mentally.', 'Fewer pieces can remove the stronger side’s winning chances.'),
    ('Choose the destination.', 'Verify the recaptures.', 'Know the resulting conversion method.'))

teach('pawn-races', (305, 310),
    ('Count checks, sacrificial promotions and king detours in a pawn race.', 'Preserve a spare pawn tempo until its purpose is clear.'),
    (
        ('A race is a sequence of turns, not two straight lines', '''Counting moves from a pawn to its last rank is only the first estimate. A promotion may give check; a king may be forced to capture the new piece and leave another pawn; a capture may change files and create a second racer. Diagram 443 gives White Kd4 with a2, d5, e6, g4 and h2 against Black Kd6 with a4, b4, f6, g5 and h7. White has connected central passers; Black can create distant flank threats. Both kings and every forcing move belong in the calculation.'''),
        ('Save the tempo that makes the later race work', '''After the best defensive ...Ke7, Kc4 must be played at once. The source contrasts h3, an apparently harmless useful-looking move that changes the final race. The shown ...b3, axb3 and ...a3 create a passer that draws White’s king toward a1. Meanwhile ...f5 and ...h5 prepare the kingside breakthrough. Keep the initial h2 square in mind throughout the replay: later Black must spend ...h4 before the files can open, and that tempo is exactly what White preserved.'''),
        ('Promotions can be sacrifices to move a king', '''Black plays ...a1=Q+ and White captures Kxa1. The temporary queen forces the white king onto a remote square, buying time for ...g4. Later White uses the same forcing idea in reverse: d6+ draws the black king to d6, then b8=Q+ draws it onto b8. The final e7 is then too far from that king to stop. Do not stop at either queen capture and conclude the sacrificed pawn failed. Each promotion changes the king’s location and therefore the result of another pawn’s race.'''),
        ('General lessons from the practical pawn endings', '''The chapter’s preceding games emphasize king routes behind the enemy pawns, reserve tempi and the comparison of central passers with passers on both wings. There is no automatic winner by pawn category: central pawns close to promotion with king support can beat distant threats, while a remote passer with check can reverse the timing. The knight endings that follow often simplify into the same race questions. In your own games count to the final useful event, not merely first promotion: include the check, compulsory capture, next pawn push and whether the resulting queen can stop the other promotion.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why is the early h3 a serious error in the source comparison?', 'It spends the tempo White needs in the later kingside and queenside race.', 'The unchanged h2 pawn forces Black to spend an extra ...h4 move later.', 'h3 exposes the white king to immediate checkmate.', 'The danger is a long-term race tempo, not an immediate mating attack.'),
        ('What is the purpose of b8=Q+ when Black can capture it?', 'To draw the king away so e7 cannot be stopped.', 'The forcing promotion is a diversion with a calculated follow-up.', 'Capturing a promoted queen is illegal.', 'The capture is legal and is exactly the king detour White wants.'),
    ),
    ('Count the forced reply after each checking promotion.', 'Keep the original reserve pawn move in view throughout a long race.'),
    ('Track both kings.', 'Include every forcing tempo.', 'Calculate beyond the first promotion.'))

teach('practical-rooks', (310, 317),
    ('Compare rook activity and king access with pawn collection.', 'Transfer theoretical methods into multi-pawn practical endings.'),
    (
        ('Activity creates threats the material count misses', '''Practical rook endings contain several pawns and changing targets, so a single theoretical diagram rarely applies immediately. An active rook can invade the seventh rank, cut off the king, check from the side and attack a pawn on the other wing. A passive rook may have more pawns but no useful move. In Chigorin–Tarrasch the source values rook activity enough to offer queenside pawns; in Lilienthal–Smyslov active king and rook play holds despite a large pawn deficit. These examples do not make pawns worthless; they show which pieces can actually use the available time.'''),
        ('Give every rook manoeuvre a target', '''Our original illustration begins with a passive Ra1 and a clear c-file. Rc1 prepares Rc7, attacking a7 and f7. Black’s ...Rb2 and ...Rxa2 collect a pawn, but White keeps an active rook. After h5 and the illustrative ...gxh5, Rc5 switches to attack h5 along the fifth rank. This is a comparison of useful roles, not a proof that White’s sequence forces a win. The new pawn target explains the sideways rook move. A rook can leave a successful invasion rank if another rank offers a better concrete job.'''),
        ('Improve before taking when counterplay is serious', '''Marshall–Chigorin shows the stronger side declining an immediate pawn capture because it would release the enemy king and rook. Centralizing the king first leads to a safer gain and zugzwang. Levenfish–Riumin combines a passed pawn, rook activity and a king journey to collect a queenside group. Marshall–Lasker demonstrates a rook transfer from file pressure to an open rank after inducing a pawn move. These are practical versions of the same question: which piece formation will let me attack a target while keeping the opponent’s counterplay restricted?'''),
        ('Defend with active resources and an evaluated exchange', '''Botvinnik–Euwe allows an enemy pawn to advance, then liquidates into a drawn rook ending through exact king placement and active rook checks. Tarrasch–Schlechter creates a passer by opening lines for the king and rook. Theoretical knowledge supplies destinations: a bridge, a sixth-rank barrier, side checks or a favourable pawn ending. After every exchange update that destination. In your games compare a pawn capture with an activating move, name the best countercheck, and only then decide. A sound review should explain the target, the opponent’s resistance and the resulting material; a final result alone does not prove that the route was accurate.'''),
    ),
    (
        ('Why might an active rook matter more than taking a pawn immediately?', 'Its checks and threats can control the king and create several targets.', 'Activity can determine whether an extra pawn is usable or merely nominal.', 'Pawn captures are forbidden in rook endings.', 'Captures are legal; the question is whether the resulting counterplay costs more.'),
        ('What makes Rc5 purposeful in the original replay?', 'It attacks the new pawn on h5 along the fifth rank.', 'The move changes the rook’s job in response to a changed pawn target.', 'The fifth rank always beats the seventh rank.', 'No rank is automatically best; concrete targets and checks decide.'),
    ),
    ('The illustrative pawn-grabbing defence is not claimed best.', 'Re-evaluate the theoretical destination after every exchange.'),
    ('Give the rook an active job.', 'Compare pawn gain with counterplay.', 'Use theory to choose the next favourable destination.'))
